/*
 * Pearson Edexcel A-Level Chemistry (9CH0) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Topic ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Every item is written as one part of a real 9CH0 structured
 * question, not as a revision flashcard: a stem (a reaction, a results table, a
 * process, an unknown, a student's method) and then the demand. Papers 1 and 2
 * (90 marks each) and Paper 3 (120 marks, synoptic and practical) all work this way,
 * and Core Practicals CP1–CP16 are examined in context, so each topic carries
 * practical items. Multiple-choice items are not in this bank.
 *
 * TARIFFS. 9CH0 parts are worth 1 to 6 marks, most 1–3, with 6-mark extended
 * open-response items. Mix per ~50-item topic: 20% 1, 28% 2, 24% 3, 14% 4, 6% 5, 8% 6.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES follow Edexcel's layout: one point per line with (1), then Allow /
 * Ignore / Do not award / TE guidance. 6-mark items give six indicative points and
 * Edexcel's split of up to 4 marks for indicative content and 2 for structure and
 * sustained reasoning.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 */

const CHEMISTRY_EDEXCEL_PRACTICE = {
  'T1': {
    name: 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics',
    questions: [
      { q: `Chromium is a d-block element with atomic number 24. Its electronic configuration does not follow the simple order of filling of sub-shells.\n\nWrite the full electronic configuration of a chromium atom, using s, p and d notation.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁵ 4s¹ (1)\nAllow 4s¹ written before 3d⁵\nAllow [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹\nDo not award 3d⁴ 4s²` },
      { q: `Gallium has two naturally occurring isotopes, ⁶⁹Ga and ⁷¹Ga. Gallium forms compounds containing the Ga³⁺ ion.\n\nState the number of electrons in one ⁷¹Ga³⁺ ion.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `28 (1)` },
      { q: `The first ionisation energy of magnesium is +738 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nWrite the equation, including state symbols, for the process that occurs when the first ionisation energy of magnesium is measured.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Mg(g) → Mg⁺(g) + e⁻ (1)\nAllow Mg(g) − e⁻ → Mg⁺(g)\nAllow e for e⁻\nState symbols on Mg and Mg⁺ required; ignore state symbol on the electron` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 3, a student titrates 25.0 cm³ of sodium carbonate solution with dilute hydrochloric acid added from a burette. Methyl orange is used as the indicator.\n\nState the colour change at the end-point.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `yellow to orange (1)\nAllow yellow to peach / yellow to first permanent orange\nDo not award yellow to red\nBoth colours needed, in the correct order` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 1, a student finds the molar volume of hydrogen by reacting a weighed length of magnesium ribbon with excess dilute hydrochloric acid and collecting the gas in a gas syringe. Before weighing, the student rubs the magnesium ribbon with emery paper.\n\nGive the reason for rubbing the magnesium ribbon with emery paper.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `to remove the (magnesium) oxide layer (from the surface) (1)\nAllow so only magnesium reacts / so the mass measured is all magnesium\nIgnore "to clean it" alone` },
      { q: `Xenon tetrafluoride, XeF₄, is a stable compound of a noble gas. The xenon atom has four bonding pairs and two lone pairs of electrons.\n\nName the shape of an XeF₄ molecule.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `square planar (1)\nDo not award square / tetrahedral / octahedral` },
      { q: `Propanone, CH₃COCH₃, is a liquid at room temperature. It contains a polar C=O bond but no O–H bond.\n\nIdentify the strongest type of intermolecular force between propanone molecules.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `permanent dipole–dipole (forces) (1)\nAllow dipole–dipole\nDo not award hydrogen bonding\nIgnore London forces mentioned in addition` },
      { q: `When aqueous silver nitrate is added to aqueous sodium chloride, a white precipitate forms.\n\nWrite the ionic equation, including state symbols, for this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s) (1)\nState symbols required\nDo not award the full equation including Na⁺ and NO₃⁻ on both sides unless spectator ions are cancelled` },
      { q: `A student uses a pipette to transfer 25.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide solution into a conical flask.\n\nCalculate the amount, in moles, of sodium hydroxide in the flask.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0.00250 (mol) (1)\nAllow 2.5 × 10⁻³ / 2.50 × 10⁻³\nIgnore significant figures` },
      { q: `The ions N³⁻, O²⁻, F⁻, Na⁺ and Mg²⁺ are isoelectronic.\n\nIdentify which of these ions has the largest ionic radius.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `N³⁻ (1)` },
      { q: `The first ionisation energies of phosphorus and sulfur are given.\nphosphorus: 1012 kJ mol⁻¹\nsulfur: 1000 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nExplain why the first ionisation energy of sulfur is lower than that of phosphorus.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `in sulfur the electron removed is from a (3p) orbital containing a pair of electrons / sulfur has a paired 3p electron but all 3p electrons in phosphorus are unpaired (1)\nrepulsion between the paired electrons makes the electron easier to remove (1)\nAllow electrons-in-boxes diagrams showing 3p³ and 3p⁴ for M1\nDo not award M2 if the explanation is based on sub-shell (s/p) difference` },
      { q: `The first five successive ionisation energies of an element X in Period 3 are shown.\n1st 578, 2nd 1817, 3rd 2745, 4th 11577, 5th 14842 (all in kJ mol⁻¹)\n\nDeduce the group of the Periodic Table that contains X. Justify your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Group 3 / Group 13 (1)\nlarge increase / big jump between the 3rd and 4th ionisation energies, so the 4th electron is removed from a shell closer to the nucleus / X has 3 outer-shell electrons (1)\nM2 dependent on M1 or on a near-miss group based on a jump` },
      { q: `A sample of gallium was analysed in a mass spectrometer. The results are shown.\n⁶⁹Ga: 60.1% abundance\n⁷¹Ga: 39.9% abundance\n\nCalculate the relative atomic mass of gallium in this sample. Give your answer to three significant figures.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[(69 × 60.1) + (71 × 39.9)] ÷ 100 (1)\n= 69.8 (1)\nCorrect answer to 3 s.f. with no working scores full marks\nAllow 69.798 for M1 only if not rounded` },
      { q: `The oxonium ion, H₃O⁺, forms when a hydrogen ion bonds to a water molecule.\n\nDraw a dot-and-cross diagram for the H₃O⁺ ion. Show outer-shell electrons only.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `three O–H shared pairs, one of which (the dative bond) has both electrons from oxygen (shown as same symbol) (1)\none lone pair on the oxygen and positive charge shown on the ion (1)\nAllow charge on O or outside brackets around the ion\nIgnore inner-shell electrons if correct` },
      { q: `Tetrachloromethane, CCl₄, and trichloromethane, CHCl₃, both have tetrahedral molecules containing polar C–Cl bonds.\n\nExplain why CCl₄ molecules are non-polar but CHCl₃ molecules are polar.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `CCl₄ is symmetrical so the bond dipoles cancel (1)\nCHCl₃ is not symmetrical / C–H bond is less polar than C–Cl so the dipoles do not cancel (1)\nAllow "dipoles cancel" and "dipoles do not cancel" in terms of vectors / centres of charge\nDo not award M1 for "CCl₄ has no polar bonds"` },
      { q: `Sodium iodide is mainly ionic, but aluminium iodide shows a significant degree of covalent character.\n\nExplain why aluminium iodide has more covalent character than sodium iodide.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Al³⁺ has a higher charge and smaller radius (than Na⁺) / Al³⁺ has a higher charge density (1)\nso Al³⁺ polarises / distorts the electron cloud of the (large) iodide ion more (1)\nDo not award M2 if the iodide ion polarises the cation` },
      { q: `The melting point of sodium is 98 °C and the melting point of magnesium is 650 °C.\n\nExplain, in terms of metallic bonding, why magnesium has a higher melting point than sodium.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Mg²⁺ ions have a greater charge (and smaller radius) than Na⁺ and/or magnesium delocalises two electrons per atom compared with one (1)\nso there is stronger (electrostatic) attraction between the cations and the delocalised electrons, requiring more energy to overcome (1)\nDo not award M2 for any reference to intermolecular forces or ionic bonds` },
      { q: `Calcium carbonate decomposes on strong heating.\nCaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g)\nA student heats 2.50 g of calcium carbonate until decomposition is complete. [Mr of CaCO₃ = 100.1; molar volume of gas at RTP = 24.0 dm³ mol⁻¹]\n\nCalculate the volume, in cm³, of carbon dioxide produced at RTP.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `amount CaCO₃ = 2.50 ÷ 100.1 = 0.02498 mol (= amount CO₂) (1)\nvolume CO₂ = 0.02498 × 24 000 = 600 cm³ (1)\nAllow 599.4 / 599 cm³\nTE on incorrect moles\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `Ethanol can be manufactured by the fermentation of glucose.\nC₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂\n[Mr: C₆H₁₂O₆ = 180.0; C₂H₅OH = 46.0]\n\nCalculate the atom economy by mass for the production of ethanol by this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(2 × 46.0) ÷ 180.0 × 100 (1)\n= 51.1% (1)\nAllow 51%\nDo not award M2 if the 2 is omitted (25.6%)\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 2, a student measures the enthalpy change of neutralisation by mixing dilute hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide solution in an expanded polystyrene cup. The value obtained is less exothermic than the data-book value.\n\nGive two reasons, other than incorrect measurements, why the experimental value is less exothermic than the data-book value.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `heat (energy) lost to the surroundings / through the open top of the cup (1)\nenergy absorbed by the cup / thermometer, or the solution does not have the specific heat capacity / density of water as assumed (1)\nAllow temperature maximum missed because the reading was taken too late\nIgnore "human error" / "inaccurate thermometer"` },
      { q: `The enthalpy change of a gas-phase reaction can be calculated using mean bond enthalpies from a data booklet. The value calculated is often different from the value found from enthalpy changes of formation.\n\nExplain why values calculated using mean bond enthalpies are different from those calculated using enthalpy changes of formation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `mean bond enthalpies are averages taken over a range of different compounds (1)\nthe actual strength of a bond depends on its environment / the actual bond enthalpy in the specific molecule differs from the mean (1)\nAllow mean bond enthalpies only apply to gaseous species, so data with liquids/solids give a different value, for either mark if not otherwise scored` },
      { q: `A 500 g sample of drinking water was found to contain 0.12 mg of lead ions.\n\nCalculate the concentration of lead ions in the water, in parts per million (ppm) by mass.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `0.12 mg = 1.2 × 10⁻⁴ g, so mass ratio = 1.2 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 500 (1)\n× 10⁶ = 0.24 ppm (1)\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `Water has a much higher boiling point than would be expected from the boiling points of the other Group 16 hydrides, because of hydrogen bonding.\n\nDraw a diagram to show a hydrogen bond between two water molecules. Include relevant lone pairs and partial charges.`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `two water molecules with δ+ on H and δ− on O shown on the molecules involved in the hydrogen bond (1)\nhydrogen bond (dashed/dotted line) from a δ+ H of one molecule to a lone pair on the O of the other, with O–H···O approximately linear (1)\nDo not award M2 if the hydrogen bond is drawn to an H atom or is within one molecule` },
      { q: `A student puts a crystal of green copper(II) chromate(VI) on a piece of moist filter paper on a microscope slide. A d.c. voltage is applied across the paper. After some time, a blue colour moves towards the negative electrode and a yellow colour moves towards the positive electrode.\n\nExplain how these observations provide evidence for the existence of ions.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the blue colour is due to Cu²⁺ ions, which are positive so are attracted to the negative electrode (cathode) (1)\nthe yellow colour is due to CrO₄²⁻ ions, which are negative so are attracted to the positive electrode (anode) (1)\nAllow the charged particles / ions move in opposite directions for 1 mark if ions not identified` },
      { q: `Chlorine consists of the isotopes ³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl in the ratio 3 : 1. A sample of chlorine gas, Cl₂, is analysed in a mass spectrometer.\n\nPredict the m/z values of the three peaks produced by Cl₂⁺ molecular ions and the ratio of their relative heights. Show your working.`, marks: 3, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `m/z 70, 72 and 74 (1)\nprobabilities: 35–35 = ¾ × ¾ = 9/16; 35–37 = 2 × ¾ × ¼ = 6/16; 37–37 = ¼ × ¼ = 1/16 (1)\nratio 70 : 72 : 74 = 9 : 6 : 1 (1)\nAllow 3 : 2 : ⅓ for M3\nCorrect ratio with correct m/z values and no working scores 2 marks` },
      { q: `Ethanol, C₂H₅OH (Mr = 46.0), and propane, C₃H₈ (Mr = 44.0), have molecules with a similar number of electrons.\nboiling point of ethanol: 78 °C\nboiling point of propane: −42 °C\n\nExplain why ethanol has a much higher boiling point than propane.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `ethanol has hydrogen bonds (between molecules) as well as London forces (1)\npropane has only London forces, which are of similar strength in both because the number of electrons is similar (1)\nhydrogen bonds are stronger so more energy is needed to separate ethanol molecules (1)\nDo not award M3 if covalent bonds are broken on boiling` },
      { q: `Silicon dioxide and carbon dioxide are both oxides of Group 14 elements.\nsilicon dioxide: melting point about 1610 °C\ncarbon dioxide: sublimes at −78 °C\n\nExplain this difference in terms of the structure and bonding of the two oxides.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `SiO₂ is a giant covalent (giant molecular) lattice (1)\nmelting SiO₂ requires many strong covalent bonds to be broken (1)\nCO₂ is simple molecular, so only weak intermolecular / London forces are overcome (1)\nDo not award M3 if covalent bonds in CO₂ are broken` },
      { q: `A student burns 4.00 g of magnesium ribbon in a crucible and obtains 6.10 g of magnesium oxide.\n2Mg(s) + O₂(g) → 2MgO(s)\n[Ar: Mg = 24.3; Mr: MgO = 40.3]\n\nCalculate the percentage yield of magnesium oxide. Give your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `amount of Mg = 4.00 ÷ 24.3 = 0.1646 mol (= amount of MgO) (1)\ntheoretical mass MgO = 0.1646 × 40.3 = 6.63 g (1)\npercentage yield = 6.10 ÷ 6.63 × 100 = 92.0% (1)\nAllow 91.9%\nTE on incorrect theoretical mass\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `A student vaporises 0.150 g of a volatile liquid in a gas syringe heated to 373 K. The volume of vapour formed is 62.4 cm³ at a pressure of 101 kPa. [R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹]\n\nCalculate the relative molecular mass of the liquid. Give your answer to three significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `conversion of units: V = 62.4 × 10⁻⁶ m³ and p = 101 000 Pa (1)\nn = pV ÷ RT = (101 000 × 62.4 × 10⁻⁶) ÷ (8.31 × 373) = 2.03 × 10⁻³ mol (1)\nMr = 0.150 ÷ 2.03 × 10⁻³ = 73.8 (1)\nTE on incorrect n\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 1, a student reacts 0.0480 g of magnesium ribbon with excess dilute hydrochloric acid at room temperature and pressure.\nMg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g)\nThe volume of hydrogen collected in a gas syringe is 46.5 cm³. [Ar: Mg = 24.3]\n\nCalculate the molar volume of hydrogen, in dm³ mol⁻¹, from these results.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `amount of Mg = 0.0480 ÷ 24.3 = 1.975 × 10⁻³ mol (1)\namount of H₂ = 1.975 × 10⁻³ mol (1:1 ratio) (1)\nmolar volume = 0.0465 ÷ 1.975 × 10⁻³ = 23.5 dm³ mol⁻¹ (1)\nAllow 23 500 cm³ mol⁻¹ for M3 only if units given as cm³ mol⁻¹\nTE on incorrect moles` },
      { q: `The amide ion, NH₂⁻, is formed when ammonia loses a proton to a very strong base.\n\nPredict the shape of the NH₂⁻ ion and its H–N–H bond angle. Justify your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `bent / V-shaped / non-linear (1)\nbond angle 104.5° (1)\nAllow 103° to 105°\nN has two bonding pairs and two lone pairs; the pairs repel to be as far apart as possible and lone pairs repel more than bonding pairs (1)` },
      { q: `The standard enthalpy changes of combustion of three substances are given.\nC₂H₄(g): −1411 kJ mol⁻¹\nH₂(g): −286 kJ mol⁻¹\nC₂H₆(g): −1560 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change for the reaction\nC₂H₄(g) + H₂(g) → C₂H₆(g)`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Hess cycle or expression: ΔH = ΣΔcH(reactants) − ΣΔcH(products) (1)\n= (−1411 + −286) − (−1560) (1)\n= −137 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\nSign required for M3\n+137 scores 2 marks\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `Mean bond enthalpies are given.\nC–H 413; C–O 358; O–H 464; O=O 498; C=O 805 (all in kJ mol⁻¹)\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change for the combustion of gaseous methanol.\nCH₃OH(g) + 1½O₂(g) → CO₂(g) + 2H₂O(g)`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `bonds broken = (3 × 413) + 358 + 464 + (1.5 × 498) = 2808 kJ (1)\nbonds made = (2 × 805) + (4 × 464) = 3466 kJ (1)\nΔH = 2808 − 3466 = −658 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\nTE on M1 and M2\nSign required for M3\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 3, a student fills a burette, reads the initial and final volumes and obtains a mean titre of 23.45 cm³. Each burette reading has an uncertainty of ±0.05 cm³. The 25.0 cm³ pipette used has an uncertainty of ±0.06 cm³.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the titre and in the pipette volume, and suggest how the student could reduce the percentage uncertainty in the titre.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `titre: (2 × 0.05) ÷ 23.45 × 100 = 0.43% (1)\npipette: 0.06 ÷ 25.0 × 100 = 0.24% (1)\nuse a more dilute solution in the burette / a more concentrated solution in the flask / a larger pipette, so that the titre is larger (1)\nAllow 0.426% for M1\nDo not award M1 for 0.21% (one reading only)\nIgnore "repeat the titration"` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 2, a student mixes 25.0 cm³ of 2.00 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid with 25.0 cm³ of 2.00 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide solution in a polystyrene cup. The temperature rises by 13.4 °C. [Assume the solution has density 1.00 g cm⁻³ and specific heat capacity 4.18 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹]\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of neutralisation, in kJ mol⁻¹.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Q = 50.0 × 4.18 × 13.4 = 2800.6 J (1)\namount of water formed = 0.0250 × 2.00 = 0.0500 mol (1)\nΔH = −2.8006 ÷ 0.0500 = −56.0 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\nSign required for M3\nDo not award M1 if 25.0 g used\nTE throughout` },
      { q: `The melting points of silicon and phosphorus are given.\nsilicon: 1414 °C\nphosphorus (white, P₄): 44 °C\n\nExplain the difference in the melting points of silicon and phosphorus in terms of structure and bonding.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `silicon has a giant covalent / giant molecular structure (1)\nmany strong covalent bonds must be broken to melt silicon (1)\nphosphorus is simple molecular (P₄) with only weak London forces between molecules, which need little energy to overcome (1)\nDo not award M3 if covalent bonds in phosphorus are broken` },
      { q: `Solid phosphorus(V) chloride does not contain PCl₅ molecules. It is an ionic solid made of PCl₄⁺ and PCl₆⁻ ions.\n\nPredict the shape and the Cl–P–Cl bond angle of each of the ions PCl₄⁺ and PCl₆⁻.`, marks: 4, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `PCl₄⁺: tetrahedral (1)\nPCl₄⁺: 109.5° (1)\nPCl₆⁻: octahedral (1)\nPCl₆⁻: 90° (1)\nAllow 109° / 109° 28′\nIgnore 180° for PCl₆⁻ if 90° also given` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 3, a student dissolves 2.65 g of anhydrous sodium carbonate in deionised water and makes the solution up to 250.0 cm³ in a volumetric flask. A 25.0 cm³ portion of this solution reacts exactly with 23.45 cm³ of dilute hydrochloric acid.\nNa₂CO₃(aq) + 2HCl(aq) → 2NaCl(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)\n[Mr: Na₂CO₃ = 106.0]\n\nCalculate the concentration, in mol dm⁻³, of the hydrochloric acid. Give your answer to three significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `amount of Na₂CO₃ in 250.0 cm³ = 2.65 ÷ 106.0 = 0.0250 mol (1)\namount in 25.0 cm³ = 0.00250 mol (1)\namount of HCl = 2 × 0.00250 = 0.00500 mol (1)\nconcentration = 0.00500 ÷ 0.02345 = 0.213 mol dm⁻³ (1)\nTE throughout\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `Propane is used as a fuel. Standard enthalpy changes of formation are given.\nC₃H₈(g): −104.5 kJ mol⁻¹\nCO₂(g): −393.5 kJ mol⁻¹\nH₂O(l): −285.8 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nWrite the equation for the complete combustion of propane, including state symbols, and use the data to calculate the standard enthalpy change of combustion of propane.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `C₃H₈(g) + 5O₂(g) → 3CO₂(g) + 4H₂O(l) (1)\nΣΔfH(products) = (3 × −393.5) + (4 × −285.8) = −2323.7 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\nΔcH = −2323.7 − (−104.5) (1)\n= −2219.2 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\nAllow −2219 kJ mol⁻¹\nSign required for final mark\nIgnore ΔfH of O₂ given as 0\nTE on incorrect ΣΔfH(products)` },
      { q: `Car airbags inflate when sodium azide, NaN₃, decomposes rapidly to form nitrogen.\n2NaN₃(s) → 2Na(s) + 3N₂(g)\nAn airbag needs 65.0 dm³ of nitrogen at 300 K and 1.20 × 10⁵ Pa. [R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹; Mr: NaN₃ = 65.0]\n\nCalculate the minimum mass of sodium azide needed. Give your answer to three significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `V = 65.0 × 10⁻³ m³ (1)\nn(N₂) = (1.20 × 10⁵ × 0.0650) ÷ (8.31 × 300) = 3.13 mol (1)\nn(NaN₃) = 3.13 × ⅔ = 2.09 mol (1)\nmass = 2.09 × 65.0 = 136 g (1)\nTE throughout\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `The boiling points of the hydrogen halides are given.\nHF: 20 °C\nHCl: −85 °C\nHBr: −67 °C\nHI: −35 °C\n\nExplain the trend in the boiling points from HCl to HI, and why HF has the highest boiling point.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `from HCl to HI the number of electrons (per molecule) increases (1)\nso London forces get stronger, and this outweighs the decrease in permanent dipole–dipole forces (1)\nHF forms hydrogen bonds between molecules because F is very electronegative and small (1)\nhydrogen bonds are stronger than the intermolecular forces in the other hydrides, so more energy is needed to separate HF molecules (1)\nDo not award M2 or M4 if covalent bonds are broken` },
      { q: `A compound Y contains carbon, hydrogen and oxygen only. Its composition by mass is C 54.5%, H 9.1%, O 36.4%. The mass spectrum of Y has its molecular ion peak at m/z = 88. [Ar: C = 12.0, H = 1.0, O = 16.0]\n\nDetermine the empirical formula and the molecular formula of Y.`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `division by Ar: C 54.5 ÷ 12.0 = 4.54; H 9.1 ÷ 1.0 = 9.1; O 36.4 ÷ 16.0 = 2.275 (1)\nratio 2 : 4 : 1, so empirical formula = C₂H₄O (1)\nempirical formula mass = 44.0, and 88 ÷ 44 = 2 (1)\nmolecular formula = C₄H₈O₂ (1)\nTE on incorrect empirical formula for M3 and M4` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 2, a student adds excess zinc powder to 50.0 cm³ of copper(II) sulfate solution in a polystyrene cup. The student records the temperature every 30 seconds for 3 minutes before adding the zinc at 3.5 minutes, then every 30 seconds for a further 6 minutes. The temperature rises quickly, reaches a maximum and then falls slowly.\n\nDescribe how the student should process these results to find the most accurate temperature change, and explain why this method is used.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `plot a graph of temperature (y-axis) against time (x-axis) (1)\ndraw a line of best fit through the points before mixing and extrapolate it forwards to 3.5 minutes / the time of addition (1)\ndraw a line of best fit through the cooling points after the maximum and extrapolate it back to 3.5 minutes; ΔT is the vertical difference between the two lines at the time of mixing (1)\nthis compensates for heat lost to the surroundings while the reaction was taking place / the maximum recorded temperature is lower than the true value (1)` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 7, a student determines the enthalpy change for the thermal decomposition of potassium hydrogencarbonate indirectly.\n2KHCO₃(s) → K₂CO₃(s) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)\nThe student adds 3.00 g of KHCO₃ to 30.0 cm³ of excess dilute hydrochloric acid. The temperature falls by 5.2 °C.\nKHCO₃(s) + HCl(aq) → KCl(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)   ΔH₂\nIn a separate experiment, the student found:\nK₂CO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → 2KCl(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)   ΔH₁ = −36.0 kJ mol⁻¹\n(data written for this practice question)\n[Mr: KHCO₃ = 100.1; specific heat capacity of solution = 4.18 J g⁻¹ °C⁻¹; density of solution = 1.00 g cm⁻³]\n\nCalculate ΔH₂ and hence the enthalpy change for the decomposition of potassium hydrogencarbonate.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Q = 30.0 × 4.18 × 5.2 = 652 J (1)\namount of KHCO₃ = 3.00 ÷ 100.1 = 0.02997 mol (1)\nΔH₂ = +0.652 ÷ 0.02997 = +21.8 kJ mol⁻¹ (positive sign because temperature falls) (1)\nHess: ΔH(decomposition) = 2ΔH₂ − ΔH₁ (1)\n= (2 × 21.8) − (−36.0) = +79.6 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\nAllow +79.4 to +79.6 (from rounding)\nTE throughout\nDo not award M3 if sign is negative` },
      { q: `A student determines the percentage of calcium carbonate in a sample of chalk. 0.500 g of the chalk is added to 50.0 cm³ of 0.200 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid (an excess). When the reaction has stopped, the excess acid is titrated with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide solution and 21.40 cm³ is needed.\nCaCO₃(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)\n[Mr: CaCO₃ = 100.1. Assume no other substance in the chalk reacts with acid.]\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of calcium carbonate in the chalk.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `initial amount of HCl = 0.0500 × 0.200 = 0.0100 mol (1)\nexcess HCl = amount of NaOH = 0.02140 × 0.100 = 0.00214 mol (1)\nHCl reacted with CaCO₃ = 0.0100 − 0.00214 = 0.00786 mol (1)\namount of CaCO₃ = 0.00786 ÷ 2 = 0.00393 mol; mass = 0.00393 × 100.1 = 0.393 g (1)\npercentage = 0.393 ÷ 0.500 × 100 = 78.7% (1)\nTE throughout\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `The standard enthalpy change of formation of ammonia, NH₃(g), is −46 kJ mol⁻¹. Bond enthalpies are given.\nN≡N: 945 kJ mol⁻¹\nH–H: 436 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nCalculate the mean bond enthalpy of the N–H bond in ammonia. Explain why this value differs slightly from the data-booklet value for the mean N–H bond enthalpy.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `½N₂(g) + 1½H₂(g) → NH₃(g), energy to break bonds = (0.5 × 945) + (1.5 × 436) = 1126.5 kJ (1)\n3 × E(N–H) = 1126.5 + 46 = 1172.5 kJ (1)\nE(N–H) = 1172.5 ÷ 3 = +391 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\nthe data-booklet value is a mean / average over N–H bonds in many different compounds (1)\nthe value calculated here applies only to the N–H bonds in ammonia, where the bonding environment is fixed (1)\nAllow 390.8 kJ mol⁻¹\nTE on arithmetic\nDo not award M2 if 46 is subtracted` },
      { q: `*The first ionisation energies of the Period 3 elements are given (kJ mol⁻¹).\nNa 496; Mg 738; Al 578; Si 789; P 1012; S 1000; Cl 1251; Ar 1521\n\nExplain the general trend in first ionisation energy across Period 3 and the deviations from this trend at aluminium and at sulfur.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 general increase across the period because nuclear charge (number of protons) increases\nIP2 electrons are added to the same shell / shielding is similar, so the outer electrons are attracted more strongly and atomic radius decreases\nIP3 Al is lower than Mg because the electron removed from Al is in a 3p sub-shell / orbital whereas in Mg it is from 3s\nIP4 the 3p electron is at a higher energy / is further from the nucleus / is partly shielded by the 3s electrons, so is easier to remove\nIP5 S is lower than P because in S the electron is removed from a 3p orbital containing two (paired) electrons\nIP6 repulsion between the paired electrons makes it easier to remove one of them (P has all 3p electrons unpaired)\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*Diamond, graphite and graphene are all forms of carbon.\nDiamond is very hard and does not conduct electricity.\nGraphite is soft, slippery and conducts electricity.\nGraphene is a single layer of carbon atoms; it is very strong and an excellent electrical conductor.\n\nDiscuss how the structure and bonding in each form of carbon account for these properties.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 diamond: each C atom covalently bonded to four other C atoms in a giant tetrahedral lattice\nIP2 diamond is hard because many strong covalent bonds must be broken, and is a non-conductor because all four outer electrons are used in bonding so there are no delocalised electrons\nIP3 graphite: each C atom bonded to three others in hexagonal layers, with one delocalised electron per atom\nIP4 graphite: weak London forces between the layers, so layers slide over each other, making it soft / slippery\nIP5 graphite conducts because the delocalised electrons can move along the layers (when a potential difference is applied)\nIP6 graphene: a single hexagonal layer with strong covalent bonds (very strong) and delocalised electrons that move across the sheet (excellent conductor)\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*In Core Practical 3, a student is given a sample of solid anhydrous sodium carbonate and a solution of hydrochloric acid of approximate concentration 0.2 mol dm⁻³. The student is asked to prepare 250.0 cm³ of a standard solution of sodium carbonate and use it to find the accurate concentration of the acid.\n\nDescribe how the student should carry out this practical to obtain accurate results.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 weigh the sodium carbonate accurately in a weighing bottle, transfer to a beaker and reweigh the bottle (weighing by difference) / rinse the bottle into the beaker\nIP2 dissolve in a small volume of deionised water, transfer to a 250.0 cm³ volumetric flask with rinsings of the beaker and glass rod\nIP3 make up to the mark with deionised water so that the bottom of the meniscus is on the line (at eye level), then stopper and invert to mix\nIP4 use a pipette (with filler) to transfer 25.0 cm³ of the sodium carbonate solution to a conical flask and add a few drops of methyl orange\nIP5 titrate with the acid from a burette, swirling, adding dropwise near the end-point, until the colour changes from yellow to orange\nIP6 repeat to obtain concordant titres (within 0.10 cm³) and use the mean of the concordant titres to calculate the concentration\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*The bond angles in four molecules are given.\nCH₄: 109.5°\nNH₃: 107°\nH₂O: 104.5°\nBF₃: 120°\n\nExplain, using electron-pair repulsion theory, the shapes of these four molecules and the differences in their bond angles.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 electron pairs (bonding and lone) around the central atom repel each other and arrange themselves to be as far apart as possible / to minimise repulsion\nIP2 CH₄ has four bonding pairs and no lone pairs, so is tetrahedral (109.5°)\nIP3 NH₃ has three bonding pairs and one lone pair, so is (trigonal) pyramidal\nIP4 H₂O has two bonding pairs and two lone pairs, so is bent / V-shaped\nIP5 lone pairs repel more strongly than bonding pairs, so each lone pair reduces the bond angle by about 2.5°, giving 107° in NH₃ and 104.5° in H₂O\nIP6 BF₃ has only three bonding pairs (and no lone pairs) around B, so is trigonal planar with 120°\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` }
    ]
  },
  'T2': {
    name: 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox',
    questions: [
      { q: `In an iodine–thiosulfate titration, thiosulfate ions are oxidised by iodine.\n2S₂O₃²⁻(aq) + I₂(aq) → S₄O₆²⁻(aq) + 2I⁻(aq)\n\nDeduce the oxidation number of sulfur in the tetrathionate ion, S₄O₆²⁻.`, marks: 1, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `+2.5 (1)\nAllow +2½ or +5/2\nDo not award 2.5 without a + sign` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 12, a student titrates a solution of iron(II) ions with a standard solution of KMnO₄ in dilute sulfuric acid.\n\nGive the systematic name of KMnO₄, including the oxidation number of manganese.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `potassium manganate(VII) (1)\nDo not award potassium permanganate\nDo not award manganate(7) or manganate(+7)` },
      { q: `The Haber process is represented by the equation\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)\n\nGive the units of the equilibrium constant, Kc, for this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `dm⁶ mol⁻² (1)\nAllow mol⁻² dm⁶\nIgnore working` },
      { q: `Calcium carbonate is heated in a sealed container. After some time the pressure of carbon dioxide becomes constant.\nCaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g)\n\nState what is meant by saying that this equilibrium is dynamic.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `the forward and reverse reactions are both still occurring at the same rate (1)\nBoth ideas needed: reactions continuing AND equal rates\nIgnore references to concentrations being constant` },
      { q: `The acid-catalysed reaction between propanone and iodine has the rate equation\nrate = k[CH₃COCH₃][H⁺]\n\nDeduce what the rate equation shows about the involvement of iodine in the rate-determining step.`, marks: 1, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `iodine is not involved in the rate-determining step / iodine reacts in a (fast) step after the rate-determining step (1)\nAllow reaction is zero order with respect to iodine so I₂ is not in the rds` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 11, a student makes an electrochemical cell from a Zn²⁺/Zn half-cell and a Cu²⁺/Cu half-cell. The two half-cells are joined by a strip of filter paper soaked in a salt solution.\n\nGive the name of a suitable salt for the solution used to soak the filter paper.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `potassium nitrate (1)\nAllow KNO₃ / saturated potassium nitrate\nAllow potassium chloride or sodium nitrate\nDo not award any salt containing Cu²⁺ or Zn²⁺` },
      { q: `Standard electrode potentials are measured relative to the standard hydrogen electrode at 298 K.\n\nState the pressure of hydrogen gas and the concentration of hydrogen ions used in the standard hydrogen electrode.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `100 kPa (H₂) AND 1.00 mol dm⁻³ H⁺(aq) (1)\nAllow 1 bar / 1 atm / 101 kPa for pressure\nBoth needed for the mark` },
      { q: `Hydrogen peroxide decomposes in the presence of a catalyst.\n2H₂O₂(aq) → 2H₂O(l) + O₂(g)\nA student found that the concentration of H₂O₂ fell from 0.80 mol dm⁻³ to 0.40 mol dm⁻³ in 9 minutes, and from 0.40 mol dm⁻³ to 0.20 mol dm⁻³ in a further 9 minutes. (data written for this practice question)\n\nDeduce the order of reaction with respect to hydrogen peroxide.`, marks: 1, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `first order, because the half-life is constant (9 minutes) (1)\nOrder and reason both needed` },
      { q: `A student determines the concentration of iodine in a solution by titration with sodium thiosulfate solution. Starch indicator is added when the solution in the flask is pale yellow.\n\nState the colour change at the end point.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `blue-black to colourless (1)\nAllow blue / dark blue / black to colourless\nDo not award to clear` },
      { q: `Acidified potassium dichromate(VI) oxidises iron(II) ions to iron(III) ions. The half-equations are\nCr₂O₇²⁻(aq) + 14H⁺(aq) + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺(aq) + 7H₂O(l)\nFe²⁺(aq) → Fe³⁺(aq) + e⁻\n\nWrite the overall ionic equation for this reaction. State symbols are not required.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6Fe²⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 6Fe³⁺ + 7H₂O\nall species correct on the correct sides (1)\ncorrectly balanced, with no electrons shown (1)\nAllow multiples\nIgnore state symbols even if incorrect` },
      { q: `Chlorine reacts with cold dilute aqueous sodium hydroxide.\nCl₂(aq) + 2NaOH(aq) → NaCl(aq) + NaClO(aq) + H₂O(l)\n\nExplain, using oxidation numbers, why this is a disproportionation reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `oxidation numbers of chlorine: 0 in Cl₂, −1 in NaCl and +1 in NaClO (1)\nchlorine (the same element) is simultaneously reduced and oxidised (1)\nSecond mark can be awarded from a description of the change 0 to −1 and 0 to +1 in the same reaction\nDo not award the second mark if the explanation refers to sodium or oxygen being oxidised` },
      { q: `Steam reacts with hot coke to form a mixture of carbon monoxide and hydrogen.\nC(s) + H₂O(g) ⇌ CO(g) + H₂(g)\n\nWrite the expression for the equilibrium constant, Kp, for this reaction and give its units.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Kp = p(CO) × p(H₂) / p(H₂O) (1)\nunits: kPa (1)\nAllow atm or Pa for units\nDo not award first mark if carbon is included in the expression\nAllow square brackets for the expression only if p is clearly shown\nTE: units mark may be awarded on an expression with the carbon term omitted but otherwise incorrect` },
      { q: `A reaction is zero order with respect to reactant B. The concentration of B is followed during the reaction.\n\nSketch a graph of [B] against time and a graph of rate against [B] for this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `[B] against time: straight line with a negative gradient (1)\nrate against [B]: horizontal straight line (1)\nAxes must be labelled for each mark\nDo not award a curve for the [B]–time graph` },
      { q: `The rate of a reaction between two gases roughly doubles when the temperature is increased from 300 K to 310 K.\n\nExplain, in terms of the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution, why a small increase in temperature causes a large increase in rate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(at higher temperature) a much greater proportion of molecules have energy greater than or equal to the activation energy (1)\nso the frequency of successful collisions increases greatly (1)\nAllow "more molecules have E ≥ Ea" for the first mark\nIgnore "collide more often" on its own — this is only a small effect\nDo not award "molecules have more activation energy"` },
      { q: `Hydrogen peroxide decomposes very slowly at room temperature, but rapidly when solid manganese(IV) oxide is added.\n\nExplain, in terms of activation energy, how manganese(IV) oxide increases the rate of this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the catalyst provides an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy (1)\nso a greater proportion of molecules have energy ≥ the (new) activation energy, giving more successful collisions per unit time (1)\nDo not award "lowers the activation energy" without reference to an alternative route for the first mark — allow if both ideas present\nIgnore references to the catalyst being heterogeneous` },
      { q: `Nitrogen monoxide reacts with hydrogen.\n2NO(g) + 2H₂(g) → N₂(g) + 2H₂O(g)\nThe rate equation is rate = k[NO]²[H₂]\n\nGive the overall order of reaction and deduce the units of the rate constant, k.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `overall order = 3 (1)\nunits of k: dm⁶ mol⁻² s⁻¹ (1)\nAllow mol⁻² dm⁶ s⁻¹ in any order` },
      { q: `Use the standard electrode potentials below.\nFe³⁺(aq) + e⁻ ⇌ Fe²⁺(aq)   E° = +0.77 V\n½I₂(aq) + e⁻ ⇌ I⁻(aq)   E° = +0.54 V\n\nCalculate E°cell for the reaction 2Fe³⁺(aq) + 2I⁻(aq) → 2Fe²⁺(aq) + I₂(aq) and state whether the reaction is thermodynamically feasible.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `E°cell = (+0.77) − (+0.54) = +0.23 V (1)\nE°cell is positive so the reaction is feasible (1)\nDo not award +0.23 V without the + sign for the first mark\nTE: allow feasibility conclusion consistent with an incorrect calculated value` },
      { q: `Use the standard electrode potentials below.\nMg²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Mg(s)   E° = −2.37 V\n2H₂O(l) + 2e⁻ ⇌ H₂(g) + 2OH⁻(aq)   E° = −0.83 V\nE°cell for the reaction of magnesium with water is +1.54 V, but a piece of magnesium ribbon placed in cold water produces almost no bubbles.\n\nGive two reasons why the reaction is much slower than the E°cell value might suggest.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `the reaction has a high activation energy / E° values give no information about rate (kinetics) (1)\nconditions are not standard (e.g. [OH⁻] not 1 mol dm⁻³) OR magnesium is coated with a layer of oxide / insoluble Mg(OH)₂ forms on the surface (1)\nAllow "kinetically stable / kinetically inert"` },
      { q: `In an alkaline hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell, the reaction at the positive electrode is\nO₂(g) + 2H₂O(l) + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻(aq)\nThe overall reaction is 2H₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2H₂O(l).\n\nWrite the half-equation for the reaction at the negative electrode.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `H₂(g) + 2OH⁻(aq) → 2H₂O(l) + 2e⁻\ncorrect species: H₂ and OH⁻ forming H₂O and electrons (1)\ncorrectly balanced (1)\nAllow multiples, e.g. 2H₂ + 4OH⁻ → 4H₂O + 4e⁻\nAllow reversible arrow\nIgnore state symbols\nDo not award an equation involving H⁺ for the first mark` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 4, a student investigates the effect of concentration on the rate of reaction between sodium thiosulfate and hydrochloric acid.\nNa₂S₂O₃(aq) + 2HCl(aq) → 2NaCl(aq) + SO₂(g) + S(s) + H₂O(l)\nThe conical flask is placed on a cross drawn on paper and the time, t, taken for the cross to disappear is measured.\n\nExplain why 1/t can be used as a measure of the rate of reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the same amount of sulfur (precipitate) is needed to obscure the cross in each experiment (1)\nrate = amount of sulfur formed ÷ time, so rate is proportional to 1/t (1)\nAllow "fixed amount of reaction occurs each time"\nDo not award "rate = 1/t" without the idea of a fixed amount of product for the second mark` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 12, a student titrates acidified iron(II) sulfate solution with potassium manganate(VII) solution in a burette. No indicator is added.\n\nExplain why no indicator is needed and state the colour change at the end point.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `MnO₄⁻ ions are (intensely) purple and are reduced to (almost) colourless Mn²⁺, so the first excess of MnO₄⁻ shows the end point / the reaction is self-indicating (1)\ncolour change: colourless (or very pale green/yellow) to (permanent) pale pink (1)\nAllow pink / light purple for end colour\nDo not award "purple to colourless" for the second mark` },
      { q: `Nitrogen dioxide dimerises in an exothermic reaction.\n2NO₂(g) ⇌ N₂O₄(g)   ΔH = −58 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nExplain the effect of increasing the temperature on the value of the equilibrium constant, Kp.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Kp decreases (1)\nthe forward reaction is exothermic so ΔS(surroundings) = −ΔH/T becomes less positive as T increases, so ΔS(total) and hence ln Kp decrease (ΔS(total) = R ln K) (1)\nAllow for the second mark: the equilibrium shifts in the endothermic (reverse) direction so the ratio of products to reactants at equilibrium falls\nDo not award "Kp decreases because pressure changes"` },
      { q: `Phosphorus(V) chloride decomposes on heating.\nPCl₅(g) ⇌ PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g)\nAn equilibrium mixture in a sealed 2.00 dm³ vessel contained 0.150 mol PCl₅, 0.0500 mol PCl₃ and 0.0500 mol Cl₂. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the value of Kc for this reaction at this temperature. Include units in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `equilibrium concentrations: [PCl₅] = 0.0750, [PCl₃] = [Cl₂] = 0.0250 mol dm⁻³ (1)\nKc = (0.0250 × 0.0250) / 0.0750 = 0.00833 / 8.33 × 10⁻³ (1)\nunits: mol dm⁻³ (1)\nTE on incorrect concentrations\nDo not award the second mark for using moles without dividing by volume (gives 0.0167)\nCorrect answer with units and no working scores 3` },
      { q: `Dinitrogen tetroxide dissociates.\nN₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g)\nAt a certain temperature, the equilibrium mixture has a total pressure of 150 kPa and the mole fraction of NO₂ is 0.40. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the value of Kp at this temperature. Include units in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `partial pressures: p(NO₂) = 0.40 × 150 = 60 kPa AND p(N₂O₄) = 0.60 × 150 = 90 kPa (1)\nKp = p(NO₂)² / p(N₂O₄) = 60² / 90 = 40 (1)\nunits: kPa (1)\nTE on incorrect partial pressures\nCorrect answer with units and no working scores 3` },
      { q: `The reaction between propanone and iodine is catalysed by hydrogen ions.\nCH₃COCH₃(aq) + I₂(aq) → CH₃COCH₂I(aq) + H⁺(aq) + I⁻(aq)\nA student measured the initial rate of reaction for four mixtures at the same temperature. (data written for this practice question)\n\nExpt | [CH₃COCH₃] / mol dm⁻³ | [I₂] / mol dm⁻³ | [H⁺] / mol dm⁻³ | initial rate / mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹\n1 | 0.40 | 0.0020 | 0.20 | 1.2 × 10⁻⁵\n2 | 0.80 | 0.0020 | 0.20 | 2.4 × 10⁻⁵\n3 | 0.80 | 0.0040 | 0.20 | 2.4 × 10⁻⁵\n4 | 0.80 | 0.0020 | 0.40 | 4.8 × 10⁻⁵\n\nDeduce the order of reaction with respect to each reactant. Justify your answers using the data.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `propanone: first order — experiments 1 and 2: [CH₃COCH₃] doubles and rate doubles (1)\niodine: zero order — experiments 2 and 3: [I₂] doubles and rate is unchanged (1)\nhydrogen ions: first order — experiments 2 and 4: [H⁺] doubles and rate doubles (1)\nEach mark needs the order and a correct comparison of data` },
      { q: `The rate constant, k, for a first-order reaction is related to temperature by the Arrhenius equation\nk = A e^(−Ea/RT)\nFor this reaction, A = 4.0 × 10¹⁰ s⁻¹ and Ea = 75 kJ mol⁻¹. (data written for this practice question)\nR = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹\n\nCalculate the value of the rate constant at 350 K. Give your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Ea converted to J mol⁻¹: 75 000 (1)\nEa/RT = 75 000 / (8.31 × 350) = 25.8 / k = 4.0 × 10¹⁰ × e^(−25.8) (1)\nk = 0.25 s⁻¹ (1)\nAllow 0.253 s⁻¹\nUnits not required, but do not award the final mark for incorrect units\nDo not award the final mark for an answer to 1 s.f.\nEa not converted to J scores max 1 (for substitution into the equation)` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 11, a student is asked to measure the emf of the cell\nZn(s) | Zn²⁺(aq) ⋮ Cu²⁺(aq) | Cu(s)\nunder standard conditions.\n\nDescribe how the student should set up the cell.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `zinc strip dipping into 1.00 mol dm⁻³ Zn²⁺(aq) (e.g. ZnSO₄) AND copper strip dipping into 1.00 mol dm⁻³ Cu²⁺(aq) (e.g. CuSO₄) in separate beakers (1)\nsalt bridge of filter paper soaked in (saturated) potassium nitrate solution dipping into both solutions (1)\nelectrodes connected by wires to a high-resistance voltmeter, at 298 K (1)\nAllow "voltmeter" without "high-resistance" for the third mark only if 298 K is stated\nIgnore cleaning the metals with emery paper` },
      { q: `Nitrogen dioxide reacts with fluorine.\n2NO₂(g) + F₂(g) → 2NO₂F(g)\nThe rate equation for the reaction is rate = k[NO₂][F₂]. The mechanism has two steps.\n\nSuggest a two-step mechanism for this reaction and identify the rate-determining step.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `step 1: NO₂ + F₂ → NO₂F + F (1)\nstep 2: NO₂ + F → NO₂F (1)\nstep 1 is the rate-determining step because it involves one NO₂ and one F₂, as in the rate equation (1)\nAllow F• for F\nThe two steps must add up to the overall equation for both of the first two marks\nAllow step 1 as NO₂ + F₂ → NO₂F₂ with step 2 NO₂F₂ + NO₂ → 2NO₂F` },
      { q: `Dinitrogen pentoxide decomposes at 45 °C.\n2N₂O₅(g) → 4NO₂(g) + O₂(g)\nThe concentration of N₂O₅ was measured at intervals. (data written for this practice question)\n\ntime / s | 0 | 500 | 1000 | 2000 | 3000\n[N₂O₅] / mol dm⁻³ | 0.240 | 0.170 | 0.120 | 0.060 | 0.030\n\nShow that the reaction is first order with respect to N₂O₅ and calculate the rate constant. Give the units of k.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `two successive half-lives identified as equal, e.g. 0.240 → 0.120 in 1000 s and 0.120 → 0.060 in 1000 s (1)\nconstant half-life, so first order (1)\nk = ln 2 / t½ = 0.693 / 1000 = 6.93 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹ (1)\nAllow 6.9 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹\nUnits required for the third mark` },
      { q: `A sealed syringe contains an equilibrium mixture of N₂O₄ and NO₂ at constant temperature.\nN₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g)\nThe plunger is pushed in, increasing the total pressure.\n\nExplain, in terms of Kp, the effect on the mole fraction of NO₂ in the new equilibrium mixture.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Kp is unchanged because the temperature is constant (1)\nwhen the pressure increases, p(NO₂)²/p(N₂O₄) increases (because the NO₂ partial pressure is squared) and is now greater than Kp (1)\nso the equilibrium shifts to the left (towards N₂O₄) until the ratio equals Kp again, so the mole fraction of NO₂ decreases (1)\nAllow "fewer moles of gas on the left" as an alternative explanation for the third mark only if Kp is shown to be unchanged` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 12, iron(II) ions are titrated with potassium manganate(VII). The solution is acidified with dilute sulfuric acid and not with hydrochloric acid.\nMnO₄⁻(aq) + 8H⁺(aq) + 5e⁻ ⇌ Mn²⁺(aq) + 4H₂O(l)   E° = +1.51 V\n½Cl₂(aq) + e⁻ ⇌ Cl⁻(aq)   E° = +1.36 V\n\nExplain, using the E° values, why hydrochloric acid is not used to acidify the solution.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `E°cell for MnO₄⁻ oxidising Cl⁻ = 1.51 − 1.36 = +0.15 V (1)\npositive so MnO₄⁻ oxidises chloride ions to chlorine (1)\nso extra MnO₄⁻ is used / the titre is too large (and toxic chlorine is released) (1)\nAllow "MnO₄⁻/Mn²⁺ has the more positive E° value" for the first mark` },
      { q: `2-bromo-2-methylpropane reacts with aqueous hydroxide ions.\n(CH₃)₃CBr + OH⁻ → (CH₃)₃COH + Br⁻\nThe rate equation is rate = k[(CH₃)₃CBr].\n\nExplain how the rate equation supports an SN1 mechanism for this reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `only the halogenoalkane appears in the rate-determining step (zero order with respect to OH⁻) (1)\nthe rate-determining step is the (heterolytic) breaking of the C–Br bond to form a (tertiary) carbocation (1)\nthe hydroxide ion attacks the carbocation in a fast second step (1)\nAllow "unimolecular rate-determining step" for the first mark` },
      { q: `A student sets up the cell Zn(s) | Zn²⁺(aq) ⋮ Cu²⁺(aq) | Cu(s). Under standard conditions E°cell = +1.10 V.\nCu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu(s)   E° = +0.34 V\nThe 1.00 mol dm⁻³ copper(II) sulfate solution is replaced with 0.10 mol dm⁻³ copper(II) sulfate solution.\n\nExplain the effect of this change on the measured cell emf.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `lower [Cu²⁺] shifts the equilibrium Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu to the left (1)\nso the electrode potential of the copper half-cell becomes less positive (1)\nso the cell emf decreases (to less than 1.10 V) (1)\nDo not award the third mark without some correct reasoning` },
      { q: `A student mixed 0.500 mol of ethanoic acid, 0.800 mol of ethanol and 0.100 mol of water with a few drops of concentrated sulfuric acid. The mixture was left to reach equilibrium at room temperature.\nCH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⇌ CH₃COOC₂H₅ + H₂O\nTitration showed that 0.180 mol of ethanoic acid remained at equilibrium. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the value of Kc for this reaction. Give your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `moles of ethanoic acid reacted = 0.320 so ethanol at equilibrium = 0.480 mol (1)\nester = 0.320 mol AND water = 0.420 mol (1)\nKc = (0.320 × 0.420) / (0.180 × 0.480) — volumes cancel (1)\nKc = 1.56 (no units) (1)\nTE throughout\nDo not award the final mark if units are given\nDo not award the second mark for water = 0.320 (initial water ignored)` },
      { q: `1.00 mol of PCl₅ was heated in a sealed container. At equilibrium, 40.0% of the PCl₅ had dissociated and the total pressure was 200 kPa. (data written for this practice question)\nPCl₅(g) ⇌ PCl₃(g) + Cl₂(g)\n\nCalculate the value of Kp at this temperature. Include units in your answer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `equilibrium moles: PCl₅ = 0.600, PCl₃ = 0.400, Cl₂ = 0.400; total = 1.40 mol (1)\nmole fractions: PCl₅ = 0.429, PCl₃ = Cl₂ = 0.286 (1)\npartial pressures: PCl₅ = 85.7 kPa, PCl₃ = Cl₂ = 57.1 kPa (1)\nKp = 57.1² / 85.7 = 38.1 kPa (1)\nTE throughout\nAllow 38 kPa\nUnits required for the final mark` },
      { q: `In Core Practical 10, a student measured the rate constant, k, of a reaction at five temperatures and plotted ln k against 1/T. The line of best fit passes through the points (3.10 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹, −4.20) and (3.40 × 10⁻³ K⁻¹, −7.80). (data written for this practice question)\nThe Arrhenius equation can be written ln k = −Ea/RT + ln A.\nR = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹\n\nCalculate the activation energy, in kJ mol⁻¹, for this reaction. Give your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `gradient = (−7.80 − (−4.20)) / (3.40 × 10⁻³ − 3.10 × 10⁻³) (1)\ngradient = −12 000 K (1)\nEa = −gradient × R = 12 000 × 8.31 = 99 720 J mol⁻¹ (1)\nEa = 99.7 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\nAllow 100 kJ mol⁻¹ (2 s.f.) for the final mark\nTE on incorrect gradient\nDo not award the final mark for a negative Ea` },
      { q: `Nitrogen monoxide reacts with hydrogen.\n2NO(g) + 2H₂(g) → N₂(g) + 2H₂O(g)\nThe initial rate of reaction was measured for three mixtures at the same temperature. (data written for this practice question)\n\nExpt | [NO] / mol dm⁻³ | [H₂] / mol dm⁻³ | initial rate / mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹\n1 | 0.0010 | 0.0040 | 2.0 × 10⁻⁵\n2 | 0.0020 | 0.0040 | 8.0 × 10⁻⁵\n3 | 0.0020 | 0.0080 | 1.6 × 10⁻⁴\n\nDeduce the rate equation for the reaction and calculate the value of the rate constant, k, including its units.`, marks: 4, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `second order with respect to NO: experiments 1 and 2, [NO] doubles and rate × 4 (1)\nfirst order with respect to H₂: experiments 2 and 3, [H₂] doubles and rate doubles (1)\nrate = k[NO]²[H₂] and k = 2.0 × 10⁻⁵ / ((0.0010)² × 0.0040) = 5000 (1)\nunits: dm⁶ mol⁻² s⁻¹ (1)\nAllow 5.0 × 10³\nTE on incorrect rate equation for the third and fourth marks` },
      { q: `A sample of gas is at temperature T₁. The temperature is then increased to T₂.\n\nSketch, on the same axes, the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution of molecular energies at T₁ and at T₂. Mark the activation energy, Ea, and use your sketch to explain why the rate of reaction is greater at T₂.`, marks: 4, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `axes labelled: number (or fraction) of molecules (y) and (kinetic) energy (x) (1)\nT₁ curve starting at the origin, single peak, approaching but not touching the energy axis at high energy (1)\nT₂ curve with lower peak displaced to the right, crossing T₁ curve once, and above T₁ at high energy (1)\nEa marked on the energy axis to the right of both peaks, and the area under the T₂ curve beyond Ea is greater, so more molecules have energy ≥ Ea and there are more successful collisions per unit time (1)\nDo not award the second mark if the curve touches the energy axis or rises again\nDo not award the third mark if the area under T₂ is clearly greater than under T₁` },
      { q: `Zinc metal is added to an acidified solution containing VO₂⁺ ions. The following data are given.\nVO₂⁺(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) + e⁻ ⇌ VO²⁺(aq) + H₂O(l)   E° = +1.00 V\nVO²⁺(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) + e⁻ ⇌ V³⁺(aq) + H₂O(l)   E° = +0.34 V\nV³⁺(aq) + e⁻ ⇌ V²⁺(aq)   E° = −0.26 V\nZn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ ⇌ Zn(s)   E° = −0.76 V\n\nDeduce, using E°cell values, the vanadium species present when the reaction is complete. Give the oxidation number of vanadium in this species.`, marks: 4, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Zn reduces VO₂⁺ to VO²⁺: E°cell = 1.00 − (−0.76) = +1.76 V (1)\nZn reduces VO²⁺ to V³⁺: E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.76) = +1.10 V (1)\nZn reduces V³⁺ to V²⁺: E°cell = −0.26 − (−0.76) = +0.50 V; all positive so all feasible (1)\nfinal species V²⁺, oxidation number +2 (1)\nAllow a statement that each E° is more positive than −0.76 V for the first three marks only if values or the comparison are clearly shown` },
      { q: `Potassium iodate(V) is used to standardise sodium thiosulfate solution. In acid, iodate(V) ions oxidise iodide ions to iodine and are themselves reduced to iodine.\n\nWrite the two ionic half-equations and use them to deduce the overall ionic equation for this reaction. Use oxidation numbers to identify the oxidising agent.`, marks: 4, command: 'Write', markScheme: `reduction: IO₃⁻ + 6H⁺ + 5e⁻ → ½I₂ + 3H₂O (1)\noxidation: I⁻ → ½I₂ + e⁻ (1)\noverall: IO₃⁻ + 5I⁻ + 6H⁺ → 3I₂ + 3H₂O (1)\nIO₃⁻ is the oxidising agent because iodine in it decreases in oxidation number from +5 to 0 (1)\nAllow multiples, e.g. 2IO₃⁻ + 12H⁺ + 10e⁻ → I₂ + 6H₂O and 2I⁻ → I₂ + 2e⁻\nIgnore state symbols\nTE: overall equation mark may be awarded from incorrect but balanced half-equations` },
      { q: `A student uses a redox titration to find the percentage of copper in a sample of brass. (data written for this practice question)\nA 1.20 g sample of brass was dissolved in nitric acid, the solution was neutralised and made up to 250.0 cm³ in a volumetric flask. A 25.0 cm³ portion was pipetted into a conical flask and excess potassium iodide solution was added.\n2Cu²⁺(aq) + 4I⁻(aq) → 2CuI(s) + I₂(aq)\nThe iodine released was titrated with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium thiosulfate solution, using starch indicator. The mean titre was 12.60 cm³.\nI₂(aq) + 2S₂O₃²⁻(aq) → 2I⁻(aq) + S₄O₆²⁻(aq)\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of copper in the brass. Give your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `moles of S₂O₃²⁻ = 0.100 × 12.60 / 1000 = 1.26 × 10⁻³ (1)\nmoles of Cu²⁺ in 25.0 cm³ = 1.26 × 10⁻³ (2Cu²⁺ ≡ I₂ ≡ 2S₂O₃²⁻, so 1:1) (1)\nmoles of Cu in 250.0 cm³ = 1.26 × 10⁻² (1)\nmass of Cu = 1.26 × 10⁻² × 63.5 = 0.800 g (1)\npercentage = 0.800 / 1.20 × 100 = 66.7 % (1)\nTE throughout\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks\nDo not award the final mark for 66.675 or 67` },
      { q: `Iron tablets contain iron(II) sulfate. In Core Practical 12, a student found the percentage by mass of iron in the tablets. (data written for this practice question)\nFive tablets, of total mass 2.15 g, were dissolved in dilute sulfuric acid and the solution was made up to 250.0 cm³. A 25.0 cm³ portion was titrated with 0.00500 mol dm⁻³ potassium manganate(VII) solution. The mean titre was 10.40 cm³.\nMnO₄⁻(aq) + 8H⁺(aq) + 5Fe²⁺(aq) → Mn²⁺(aq) + 5Fe³⁺(aq) + 4H₂O(l)\n\nCalculate the percentage by mass of iron in the tablets. Give your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `moles of MnO₄⁻ = 0.00500 × 10.40 / 1000 = 5.20 × 10⁻⁵ (1)\nmoles of Fe²⁺ in 25.0 cm³ = 5 × 5.20 × 10⁻⁵ = 2.60 × 10⁻⁴ (1)\nmoles of Fe²⁺ in 250.0 cm³ = 2.60 × 10⁻³ (1)\nmass of Fe = 2.60 × 10⁻³ × 55.8 = 0.145 g (1)\npercentage = 0.145 / 2.15 × 100 = 6.75 % (1)\nTE throughout\nAllow 6.7 % or 6.8 %\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `In the Contact process, sulfur dioxide is oxidised to sulfur trioxide.\n2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)\nA mixture of 2.00 mol SO₂ and 1.00 mol O₂ was allowed to reach equilibrium at constant temperature. At equilibrium, 1.80 mol SO₃ was present and the total pressure was 250 kPa. (data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the value of Kp for this reaction. Include units in your answer.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `equilibrium moles: SO₂ = 0.20, O₂ = 0.10, SO₃ = 1.80 (1)\ntotal moles = 2.10 and mole fractions SO₂ = 0.0952, O₂ = 0.0476, SO₃ = 0.857 (1)\npartial pressures: SO₂ = 23.8 kPa, O₂ = 11.9 kPa, SO₃ = 214 kPa (1)\nKp = p(SO₃)² / (p(SO₂)² × p(O₂)) = 214.3² / (23.8² × 11.9) = 6.80 (1)\nunits: kPa⁻¹ (1)\nTE throughout\nAllow 6.8 kPa⁻¹\nAllow answers in range 6.78 to 6.82 from rounded partial pressures` },
      { q: `*The reaction between propanone and iodine in acid solution can be followed by a continuous monitoring method.\nCH₃COCH₃(aq) + I₂(aq) → CH₃COCH₂I(aq) + H⁺(aq) + I⁻(aq)\nThe propanone and the sulfuric acid catalyst are used in large excess. Samples of the mixture are removed at intervals and the remaining iodine is found by titration.\n\nDescribe how this experiment should be carried out, and how the results are used to show that the reaction is zero order with respect to iodine.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 mix measured volumes of propanone and sulfuric acid, add a measured volume of iodine solution and start the timer immediately\nIP2 at regular time intervals withdraw a sample (e.g. 10.0 cm³) using a pipette\nIP3 add the sample to sodium hydrogencarbonate solution to quench the reaction (neutralises the acid catalyst)\nIP4 titrate the remaining iodine with standard sodium thiosulfate solution, adding starch near the end point\nIP5 titre is proportional to [I₂]; plot titre (or [I₂]) against time; propanone and acid in excess so their concentrations stay (approximately) constant\nIP6 a straight line (constant gradient) shows the rate does not change as [I₂] falls, so zero order with respect to iodine\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*In Core Practical 10, a student plans to find the activation energy of the reaction between sodium thiosulfate solution and hydrochloric acid. The reaction produces a precipitate of sulfur.\nNa₂S₂O₃(aq) + 2HCl(aq) → 2NaCl(aq) + SO₂(g) + S(s) + H₂O(l)\n\nDescribe how the student should carry out the experiment and process the results to determine the activation energy.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 use the same volumes and concentrations of sodium thiosulfate and hydrochloric acid in every experiment; warm the solutions separately in a water bath to the required temperature\nIP2 mix in a conical flask placed over a cross on paper and start the timer; record the time, t, for the cross to disappear\nIP3 repeat at five or more different temperatures (e.g. between 20 °C and 60 °C), measuring the temperature of the mixture with a thermometer (e.g. mean of start and end values)\nIP4 rate (and hence k) is proportional to 1/t; convert temperatures to kelvin\nIP5 plot ln(1/t) against 1/T and draw a line of best fit\nIP6 gradient = −Ea/R, so Ea = −gradient × 8.31 (J mol⁻¹); also accept: carry out in a fume cupboard / well-ventilated room because SO₂ is toxic\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*In Core Practical 11, a student is asked to measure the standard electrode potential of the Fe³⁺(aq)/Fe²⁺(aq) half-cell.\n\nDescribe how the student should set up the apparatus, including the reference electrode, and explain how the measurement gives the value of E°.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 platinum electrode dipping into a solution containing both Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ ions, each at 1.00 mol dm⁻³ (equal concentrations)\nIP2 connected to a standard hydrogen electrode: hydrogen gas at 100 kPa bubbled over a platinum electrode in 1.00 mol dm⁻³ H⁺(aq)\nIP3 salt bridge of filter paper soaked in saturated potassium nitrate solution connecting the two solutions\nIP4 electrodes connected to a high-resistance voltmeter so that (almost) no current flows\nIP5 temperature 298 K throughout (standard conditions)\nIP6 E°(SHE) is defined as 0.00 V, so the emf measured equals E° of the Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ half-cell, with the platinum electrode as the positive terminal (E° = +0.77 V); allow use of a secondary standard of known E°, e.g. Cu²⁺/Cu, with E° = Ecell + E°(reference)\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*In the Contact process, sulfur dioxide is converted to sulfur trioxide.\n2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g)   ΔH = −196 kJ mol⁻¹\nTypical operating conditions are a temperature of about 450 °C, a pressure of 100–200 kPa and a vanadium(V) oxide catalyst.\n\nDiscuss the choice of these conditions. Your answer should refer to rate, equilibrium yield, the equilibrium constant and cost.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 the forward reaction is exothermic, so a lower temperature increases the value of Kp and the equilibrium yield of SO₃\nIP2 at low temperature the rate is too slow, so about 450 °C is a compromise between rate and yield\nIP3 there are fewer moles of gas on the right, so higher pressure would shift the equilibrium to the right (Kp unchanged, only temperature changes Kp)\nIP4 conversion is already very high (about 99 %) at low pressure, so the extra cost/energy/safety risk of high pressure is not justified\nIP5 the V₂O₅ catalyst provides an alternative route with lower activation energy so increases the rate, allowing a lower temperature; it has no effect on Kp or the equilibrium yield\nIP6 it is a heterogeneous catalyst (solid with gaseous reactants), so it is easily separated / reused; unreacted gases can be recycled and excess air (O₂) shifts the equilibrium to the right\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` }
    ]
  },
  'T3': {
    name: 'Inorganic Chemistry',
    questions: [
      { q: `Strontium nitrate is used in fireworks to produce a coloured flame.\n\nState the colour of the flame produced by strontium ions.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `red / crimson (1)\nAllow scarlet\nDo not award brick-red or orange-red (calcium)` },
      { q: `Tollens' reagent contains the complex ion [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺.\n\nState the shape of this complex ion.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `linear (1)\nAllow bond angle 180° if shape named\nIgnore "planar"` },
      { q: `A student adds concentrated hydrochloric acid dropwise to a blue solution of copper(II) sulfate until no further change is seen. The solution turns yellow-green.\n\nGive the formula of the copper-containing complex ion responsible for the yellow-green colour.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `[CuCl₄]²⁻ (1)\nCharge must be shown\nIgnore missing square brackets` },
      { q: `Potassium manganate(VII), KMnO₄, is used in redox titrations to determine the concentration of iron(II) ions.\n\nState the oxidation number of manganese in the manganate(VII) ion and in the manganese-containing product of the titration.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `+7 in MnO₄⁻ and +2 in Mn²⁺ (1)\nBoth needed for the mark\nAllow 7+ and 2+` },
      { q: `EDTA⁴⁻ forms a very stable 1 : 1 complex with Ca²⁺ ions, and is used to treat lead poisoning by forming a similar complex with Pb²⁺ ions.\n\nIdentify the type of ligand that EDTA⁴⁻ is.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `hexadentate / multidentate (1)\nAllow "forms six dative bonds to the metal ion"\nDo not award bidentate` },
      { q: `A few drops of silver nitrate solution, acidified with dilute nitric acid, are added to a solution of potassium iodide.\n\nState what is observed.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `yellow precipitate (1)\nBoth colour and "precipitate"/"solid" needed\nAllow pale yellow\nDo not award cream` },
      { q: `Chromium has atomic number 24.\n\nGive the full electronic configuration of a chromium atom in its ground state.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁵ 4s¹ (1)\nAllow 4s¹ written before 3d⁵\nDo not award [Ar] notation if the question asks for full configuration\nDo not award 3d⁴ 4s²` },
      { q: `In haemoglobin, an Fe²⁺ ion is bonded to four nitrogen atoms of a haem group and to a nitrogen atom of the globin protein. The sixth position is taken by an O₂ molecule, which can be replaced by carbon monoxide.\n\nState the type of bond formed between the O₂ molecule and the Fe²⁺ ion.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `dative (covalent) bond / coordinate bond (1)\nIgnore "covalent" alone\nDo not award ionic` },
      { q: `In the Contact process, sulfur dioxide is oxidised to sulfur trioxide over a heterogeneous catalyst at about 450 °C.\n\nName the catalyst used.`, marks: 1, command: 'Name', markScheme: `vanadium(V) oxide (1)\nAllow V₂O₅ / vanadium pentoxide\nDo not award vanadium alone` },
      { q: `To test a solution for sulfate ions, a student adds dilute hydrochloric acid followed by barium chloride solution. A white precipitate forms.\n\nWrite the ionic equation, including state symbols, for the formation of the white precipitate, and explain why the hydrochloric acid is added first.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Ba²⁺(aq) + SO₄²⁻(aq) → BaSO₄(s) (1)\nState symbols required\nto react with / remove carbonate (or sulfite) ions, which would also give a white precipitate with Ba²⁺ (1)\nDo not award sulfuric acid as the acid used` },
      { q: `Sodium hydroxide solution is added to a pale green solution of iron(II) sulfate. A green precipitate forms. After the mixture is left to stand in air, the surface of the precipitate turns orange-brown.\n\nExplain these observations.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `green precipitate is Fe(OH)₂ / Fe(OH)₂(H₂O)₄ (1)\nAllow [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(OH)₂(H₂O)₄ + 2H₂O\nthe iron(II) is oxidised by oxygen in the air to iron(III) hydroxide / Fe(OH)₃ (1)\nAllow Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺ by air / O₂` },
      { q: `Sodium hydroxide solution is added dropwise to zinc sulfate solution. A white precipitate forms, which dissolves when excess sodium hydroxide solution is added to give a colourless solution.\n\nWrite the equation for the dissolving of the precipitate and state the property of zinc hydroxide that this reaction shows.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Zn(OH)₂ + 2OH⁻ → [Zn(OH)₄]²⁻ (1)\nAllow Zn(OH)₂(H₂O)₄ + 2OH⁻ → [Zn(OH)₄]²⁻ + 4H₂O\nAllow equations forming [Zn(OH)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁻\n(zinc hydroxide is) amphoteric (1)` },
      { q: `Excess concentrated aqueous ammonia is added to a solution containing [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ ions. A pale blue precipitate forms first, which then dissolves to give a deep blue solution.\n\nWrite the equation for the formation of the deep blue complex ion from the pale blue precipitate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Cu(OH)₂(H₂O)₄ + 4NH₃ → [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ + 2OH⁻ + 2H₂O\ncorrect complex [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ (1)\nrest of equation correct and balanced (1)\nAllow Cu(OH)₂ + 4NH₃ + 2H₂O → [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ + 2OH⁻\nDo not award [Cu(NH₃)₆]²⁺` },
      { q: `The compound [Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl₂ dissolves in water to form a purple solution. When excess silver nitrate solution is added, only two of the three chloride ions are precipitated.\n\nDeduce the oxidation number of cobalt and the coordination number of the cobalt ion in this compound.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `oxidation number +3 (1)\ncoordination number 6 (1)\nAllow 3+ for oxidation number` },
      { q: `A student is analysing an unknown white solid, which may be an ammonium salt.\n\nDescribe a test to show that the solid contains ammonium ions, giving the positive result.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `add (aqueous) sodium hydroxide and warm (1)\ngas turns damp red litmus paper blue (1)\nAllow damp universal indicator paper turns blue/purple\nAllow dense white smoke with concentrated HCl on a glass rod for the second mark\nSecond mark is dependent on use of an alkali` },
      { q: `In the Haber process, the iron catalyst must be protected from sulfur compounds present as impurities in the natural gas feedstock.\n\nExplain why sulfur compounds reduce the effectiveness of the catalyst.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `sulfur (compounds) adsorb (strongly) on to the surface / active sites of the iron (1)\nblocking the active sites so reactants (N₂ and H₂) cannot adsorb / reducing the surface area available (1)\nAllow "catalyst is poisoned" for the first mark only if adsorption idea is given` },
      { q: `The boiling temperatures of three halogens are given.\n\nchlorine −34 °C\nbromine 59 °C\niodine 184 °C\n\nExplain the trend in boiling temperature from chlorine to iodine.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `number of electrons (in the molecule) increases down the group (1)\nso London forces / instantaneous dipole–induced dipole forces between molecules are stronger and need more energy to overcome (1)\nDo not award breaking covalent bonds\nAllow van der Waals forces for London forces` },
      { q: `Magnesium reacts very slowly with cold water, but barium reacts vigorously with cold water.\n\nExplain why reactivity increases down Group 2.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `atomic radius increases / outer electrons are further from the nucleus / more shielding (1)\nso the first and second ionisation energies decrease / outer s electrons are lost more easily (1)\nAllow less attraction between nucleus and outer electrons\nIgnore references to nuclear charge increasing unless shielding is also mentioned` },
      { q: `Chlorine is added to drinking water in small amounts to kill bacteria. Chlorine reacts with water in a reversible reaction.\n\nWrite the equation for this reaction and give the oxidation number of chlorine in the chlorine-containing product that kills bacteria.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HClO (1)\nAllow ⇌ HCl + HOCl / H⁺ + Cl⁻ + HClO\nAllow → for ⇌\n+1 (in HClO / chloric(I) acid) (1)` },
      { q: `Acidified potassium dichromate(VI) is used to oxidise primary alcohols. During the reaction, the solution changes colour from orange to green.\n\nWrite the ionic half-equation for the reduction of dichromate(VI) ions in acidic solution.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 14H⁺ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 7H₂O\ncorrect species on both sides (1)\nbalanced, including electrons (1)\nIgnore state symbols` },
      { q: `When sodium carbonate solution is added to a solution of iron(III) chloride, effervescence is seen and a brown precipitate forms. No effervescence is seen when sodium carbonate solution is added to iron(II) sulfate solution.\n\nExplain why effervescence occurs with the iron(III) solution.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `[Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ is acidic / releases H⁺ ions because the small, highly charged Fe³⁺ ion polarises the O–H bonds in the water ligands (1)\nAllow [Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + H₂O ⇌ [Fe(H₂O)₅(OH)]²⁺ + H₃O⁺\nthe H⁺ / H₃O⁺ ions react with carbonate ions to produce carbon dioxide (1)\nAllow 2H⁺ + CO₃²⁻ → CO₂ + H₂O` },
      { q: `In the core practical to prepare chromium(II) ethanoate, the red precipitate is filtered off under suction and washed, in turn, with deoxygenated water, ethanol and ethoxyethane (ether). The product is then dried quickly.\n\nExplain why the product is washed with ethanol and then ethoxyethane.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `ethanol removes the water (and ethoxyethane removes the ethanol) (1)\nethoxyethane is very volatile so the solid dries rapidly, reducing the time it is exposed to (oxygen in) air, which would oxidise Cr(II) to Cr(III) (1)\nAllow "evaporates quickly" for volatile` },
      { q: `Iron and zinc are both d-block elements, but only iron is classified as a transition metal.\n\nIron, atomic number 26; zinc, atomic number 30.\n\nGive the electronic configurations of an iron atom and an Fe³⁺ ion, using [Ar] notation, and explain why zinc is not a transition metal.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Fe: [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s² (1)\nFe³⁺: [Ar] 3d⁵ (1)\nzinc only forms the Zn²⁺ ion, which has a full 3d sub-shell / 3d¹⁰ (so does not form any ion with an incomplete d sub-shell) (1)\nAllow 4s written before 3d\nDo not award "zinc has a full d sub-shell" without reference to the ion` },
      { q: `Aqueous copper(II) sulfate is blue but aqueous zinc sulfate is colourless. Both contain octahedral hexaaqua ions.\n\nExplain this difference.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `in [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ the d-orbitals are split into two energy levels by the ligands (1)\nan electron is promoted to the higher level by absorbing a frequency of visible light, and the remaining (complementary) colour is transmitted/reflected (1)\nZn²⁺ is 3d¹⁰ / has a full d sub-shell, so no electron can be promoted and no visible light is absorbed (1)\nAllow Cu²⁺ is 3d⁹ / has a partially filled d sub-shell\nDo not award references to electrons falling back and emitting light` },
      { q: `Potassium chromate(VI) solution and potassium dichromate(VI) solution are related by the equilibrium\n\n2CrO₄²⁻(aq) + 2H⁺(aq) ⇌ Cr₂O₇²⁻(aq) + H₂O(l)\n\nA student adds sodium hydroxide solution to orange potassium dichromate(VI) solution.\n\nState and explain the colour change seen, and deduce whether this is a redox reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `orange to yellow (1)\nOH⁻ ions remove H⁺ ions / react to form water, so the equilibrium shifts to the left, forming CrO₄²⁻ (1)\nnot redox because chromium is +6 in both CrO₄²⁻ and Cr₂O₇²⁻ (1)\nThird mark requires both oxidation numbers or a statement that the oxidation number of Cr is unchanged at +6` },
      { q: `A few drops of sodium hydroxide solution are added to a very pale pink solution of manganese(II) sulfate.\n\nDescribe what is seen immediately and on standing in air, and give an equation for the initial reaction.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `off-white / pale brown precipitate (1)\nAllow cream / beige\n[Mn(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Mn(OH)₂(H₂O)₄ + 2H₂O (1)\nAllow Mn²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Mn(OH)₂\nprecipitate darkens / turns brown on standing as it is oxidised by oxygen in the air (1)` },
      { q: `Aqueous ammonia is added dropwise and then in excess to a pink solution of cobalt(II) chloride. The final solution is left to stand in air.\n\nDescribe the observations you would make.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `blue (or blue-green) precipitate with a few drops of ammonia (1)\nprecipitate dissolves in excess (concentrated) ammonia to give a pale brown / straw-coloured solution (1)\nAllow yellow-brown\nsolution darkens (to brown) on standing in air, as Co(II) is oxidised to Co(III) / [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ (1)\nDo not award pink precipitate` },
      { q: `When a sample of a sodium compound is held in a roaring Bunsen flame on a nichrome wire, a persistent yellow flame is seen.\n\nExplain how the yellow colour is produced.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(heat energy) excites / promotes electrons to higher energy levels (1)\nthe electrons fall back (drop) to lower energy levels (1)\nreleasing energy as light of a specific frequency (ΔE = hf) in the visible region / yellow part of the spectrum (1)\nIgnore references to the metal burning` },
      { q: `A student adds chlorine water to separate test tubes containing potassium bromide solution and potassium iodide solution. A few drops of cyclohexane are then added to each tube, and each tube is shaken and allowed to settle.\n\nState the colour of the cyclohexane layer in each tube, write the ionic equation for the reaction with potassium bromide, and explain why the reactions occur.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `bromide: orange (organic layer) and iodide: purple / violet (organic layer) (1)\nBoth needed\nAllow orange-red / red-brown for bromine\nCl₂ + 2Br⁻ → 2Cl⁻ + Br₂ (1)\nchlorine is a stronger oxidising agent than bromine and iodine / chlorine has a greater tendency to gain electrons (1)\nAllow chlorine is more reactive than bromine and iodine` },
      { q: `Lithium nitrate decomposes on heating in a similar way to magnesium nitrate, but sodium nitrate decomposes differently.\n\nWrite equations for the thermal decomposition of lithium nitrate and sodium nitrate, and suggest why lithium nitrate behaves like a Group 2 nitrate.`, marks: 3, command: 'Write', markScheme: `4LiNO₃ → 2Li₂O + 4NO₂ + O₂ (1)\n2NaNO₃ → 2NaNO₂ + O₂ (1)\nAllow multiples\nIgnore state symbols\nLi⁺ is very small / has a high charge density (similar to Mg²⁺) so it polarises the nitrate ion strongly (1)` },
      { q: `In the core practical to determine the iron content of iron tablets, the tablets are dissolved in excess dilute sulfuric acid, and portions of the solution are titrated with potassium manganate(VII) solution.\n\nMnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺\n\nExplain why excess dilute sulfuric acid is used, why no indicator is added, and why hydrochloric acid is not used instead of sulfuric acid.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(excess) H⁺ ions are needed for the reduction of MnO₄⁻ (to Mn²⁺) / to prevent formation of brown MnO₂ (1)\nMnO₄⁻ is (deep) purple and Mn²⁺ is (almost) colourless, so the end point is the first permanent pale pink colour (1)\nCl⁻ ions would be oxidised by MnO₄⁻ (to chlorine), so the titre would be too high (1)\nAllow "self-indicating" for the second mark only if the colour change is described` },
      { q: `When concentrated hydrochloric acid is added to a solution containing [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺, the following ligand exchange occurs.\n\n[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4Cl⁻ ⇌ [CuCl₄]²⁻ + 6H₂O\n\nGive the shape of each complex ion, with its bond angle, and explain why the coordination number changes.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺: octahedral, 90° (1)\n[CuCl₄]²⁻: tetrahedral, 109.5° (1)\nAllow 109° or 109½°\nCl⁻ ligands are larger than H₂O ligands, so only four fit around the Cu²⁺ ion (1)` },
      { q: `In the core practical to prepare chromium(II) ethanoate, a student used 5.00 g of sodium dichromate(VI), Na₂Cr₂O₇·2H₂O (Mr = 298.0), and obtained 3.62 g of dry product, Cr₂(CH₃COO)₄(H₂O)₂ (Mr = 376.0). One mole of dichromate(VI) ions produces one mole of product.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the percentage yield. Give your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `moles Na₂Cr₂O₇·2H₂O = 5.00 ÷ 298.0 = 0.016779 mol (1)\ntheoretical mass of product = 0.016779 × 376.0 = 6.309 g (1)\npercentage yield = 3.62 ÷ 6.309 × 100 = 57.4% (1)\nAnswer must be to 3 s.f.\nTE throughout\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `Chlorine is bubbled through cold dilute aqueous sodium hydroxide to make household bleach. When chlorine is bubbled through hot concentrated aqueous sodium hydroxide instead, sodium chlorate(V) is formed.\n\nWrite ionic equations for both reactions and use oxidation numbers to explain why the reaction with hot alkali is a disproportionation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `cold: Cl₂ + 2OH⁻ → Cl⁻ + ClO⁻ + H₂O (1)\nhot: 3Cl₂ + 6OH⁻ → 5Cl⁻ + ClO₃⁻ + 3H₂O (1)\nAllow full equations with NaOH\noxidation numbers: Cl₂ 0, Cl⁻ −1, ClO₃⁻ +5 (1)\nchlorine is simultaneously reduced (0 to −1) and oxidised (0 to +5) (1)\nFourth mark can be awarded from correct oxidation numbers in the cold reaction (0, −1, +1) if hot equation is wrong` },
      { q: `Cisplatin, [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂], is a square planar complex used as an anticancer drug. Its isomer, transplatin, is not an effective drug.\n\nExplain how cisplatin acts as an anticancer drug and why transplatin is not effective.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(inside the cell) chloride ligands are replaced by water / ligand exchange occurs (1)\nplatinum then bonds (datively) to nitrogen atoms of guanine bases in DNA (1)\nthe two adjacent cis positions bind to two bases on the same strand, distorting the DNA and preventing replication / cell division (1)\nin transplatin the two Cl⁻ ligands are opposite each other (180°), so it cannot bind to adjacent bases in the same way (1)\nIgnore references to side effects` },
      { q: `EDTA⁴⁻ reacts with hexaaquacopper(II) ions as shown.\n\n[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺(aq) + EDTA⁴⁻(aq) → [Cu(EDTA)]²⁻(aq) + 6H₂O(l)\n\nThe enthalpy change for this reaction is very small.\n\nExplain, in terms of entropy and free energy, why the complex [Cu(EDTA)]²⁻ is much more stable than [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2 particles / moles on the left form 7 on the right (1)\nso ΔS(system) is (large and) positive (1)\nΔH is approximately zero because six Cu–O bonds are broken and six similar dative bonds are formed (1)\nso ΔG (= ΔH − TΔS) is negative / the equilibrium constant is very large and the reaction is feasible (1)\nAllow "chelate effect" alone for no marks` },
      { q: `In the core practical to prepare chromium(II) ethanoate, zinc granules and concentrated hydrochloric acid are added to sodium dichromate(VI) solution in a flask fitted with a tube that lets hydrogen escape but keeps air out. The solution changes colour from orange, to green, and finally to blue.\n\nExplain the colour changes and why air must be excluded.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `orange is Cr₂O₇²⁻ (+6) which is reduced to green Cr³⁺ (+3) (1)\nthen Cr³⁺ is reduced to blue Cr²⁺ (+2) (1)\nzinc is the reducing agent: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ (1)\nAllow zinc reacts with acid to form hydrogen, which displaces air\nCr²⁺ is easily oxidised (back to Cr³⁺) by oxygen in the air (1)` },
      { q: `A student adds a small piece of barium to water in a beaker. The reaction is vigorous. The student then tries to react a piece of barium with dilute sulfuric acid, but the reaction stops very quickly.\n\nWrite an equation for the reaction of barium with water, give two observations, and explain why the solution formed has a higher pH than the solution formed when magnesium reacts with water. Suggest why the reaction with dilute sulfuric acid stops.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Ba + 2H₂O → Ba(OH)₂ + H₂ (1)\nIgnore state symbols\nany two from: effervescence / fizzing; barium dissolves / gets smaller; colourless solution forms; beaker gets warm (1)\nBa(OH)₂ is more soluble than Mg(OH)₂ so a higher concentration of OH⁻ ions forms (1)\nbarium sulfate is insoluble and forms a layer on the barium, preventing further reaction (1)` },
      { q: `Dilute aqueous ammonia is added dropwise to a green solution containing [Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺ ions until a precipitate forms. Excess concentrated aqueous ammonia is then added.\n\nState the observations and give the equations for both reactions.`, marks: 4, command: 'Give', markScheme: `green precipitate (1)\n[Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + 3NH₃ → Cr(OH)₃(H₂O)₃ + 3NH₄⁺ (1)\nAllow equation using OH⁻\nprecipitate dissolves (in excess) to give a purple solution (1)\nAllow violet\nCr(OH)₃(H₂O)₃ + 6NH₃ → [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺ + 3OH⁻ + 3H₂O (1)` },
      { q: `A student is given three unlabelled solutions. One contains sodium chloride, one sodium bromide and one sodium iodide.\n\nDescribe how the student could use silver nitrate solution, dilute nitric acid and aqueous ammonia to identify each solution. Include the expected observations.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `acidify each with dilute nitric acid, then add silver nitrate solution (1)\nAllow nitric acid removes carbonate/hydroxide ions which would give other precipitates\nchloride: white precipitate; bromide: cream precipitate; iodide: yellow precipitate (1)\nadd dilute aqueous ammonia: only the white (AgCl) precipitate dissolves (1)\nadd concentrated aqueous ammonia: the cream (AgBr) precipitate dissolves, the yellow (AgI) precipitate does not (1)\nDo not award hydrochloric acid for acidification` },
      { q: `A student determined the mass of iron in iron tablets. Five tablets were dissolved in excess dilute sulfuric acid and the solution was made up to 250.0 cm³ in a volumetric flask. 25.0 cm³ portions were titrated with 0.0100 mol dm⁻³ potassium manganate(VII) solution.\n\nMnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺\n\n(data written for this practice question)\nTitre / cm³: rough 12.10; run 1 11.65; run 2 11.60; run 3 11.70\n\nCalculate the mass, in mg, of iron in one tablet. Give your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.\n[Ar: Fe = 55.8]`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mean titre = 11.65 cm³ (from runs 1–3, rough excluded) (1)\nmoles MnO₄⁻ = 0.0100 × 11.65 ÷ 1000 = 1.165 × 10⁻⁴ mol (1)\nmoles Fe²⁺ in 25.0 cm³ = 5 × 1.165 × 10⁻⁴ = 5.825 × 10⁻⁴ mol (1)\nmoles Fe²⁺ in 250.0 cm³ = 5.825 × 10⁻³ mol, so per tablet = 1.165 × 10⁻³ mol (1)\nmass per tablet = 1.165 × 10⁻³ × 55.8 = 0.0650 g = 65.0 mg (1)\nAnswer to 3 s.f.\nTE on incorrect moles\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `Standard electrode potentials are given.\n\nVO₂⁺ + 2H⁺ + e⁻ ⇌ VO²⁺ + H₂O   E° = +1.00 V\nVO²⁺ + 2H⁺ + e⁻ ⇌ V³⁺ + H₂O   E° = +0.34 V\nV³⁺ + e⁻ ⇌ V²⁺   E° = −0.26 V\nSO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ SO₂ + 2H₂O   E° = +0.17 V\nZn²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Zn   E° = −0.76 V\n\nA yellow solution of VO₂⁺ ions is shaken with excess zinc in acid. A second sample is treated with excess sulfur dioxide.\n\nUse the E° values to deduce the final vanadium species formed in each case, and state the colour of each final solution.`, marks: 5, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `zinc: E°cell for reduction of VO₂⁺ to VO²⁺ = +1.76 V and VO²⁺ to V³⁺ = +1.10 V (both positive so feasible) (1)\nV³⁺ to V²⁺: E°cell = −0.26 − (−0.76) = +0.50 V, so also feasible (1)\nfinal species V²⁺, violet / purple solution (1)\nsulfur dioxide: VO₂⁺ to VO²⁺ (+0.83 V) and VO²⁺ to V³⁺ (+0.17 V) feasible, but V³⁺ to V²⁺ = −0.43 V so not feasible (1)\nfinal species V³⁺, green solution (1)\nAllow E°cell values described as "E° of reducing agent more negative than E° of vanadium couple"\nIgnore references to kinetics` },
      { q: `A pale green crystalline solid, Y, is analysed. The following results are obtained.\n\nTest 1: Aqueous sodium hydroxide is added to a solution of Y. A green precipitate forms which turns orange-brown at the surface on standing.\nTest 2: Dilute hydrochloric acid and barium chloride solution are added to a solution of Y. A white precipitate forms.\nTest 3: Solid Y is warmed with aqueous sodium hydroxide. A gas is given off which turns damp red litmus paper blue.\n\nDeduce the ions present in Y, explaining your reasoning, and suggest a formula for Y.`, marks: 5, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `Test 1: Fe²⁺ (green precipitate of Fe(OH)₂) (1)\nturning orange-brown as Fe(OH)₂ is oxidised (by air) to Fe(OH)₃ (1)\nTest 2: SO₄²⁻ (white precipitate of BaSO₄; acid rules out carbonate) (1)\nTest 3: NH₄⁺ (alkaline gas is NH₃) (1)\nY is (NH₄)₂Fe(SO₄)₂ / ammonium iron(II) sulfate (1)\nAllow hydrated formula (NH₄)₂Fe(SO₄)₂·6H₂O\nAllow ionic equations as explanation` },
      { q: `*A student plans to compare the thermal stability of magnesium nitrate, calcium nitrate and barium nitrate. Each nitrate decomposes on heating to give the metal oxide, nitrogen dioxide and oxygen.\n\nDiscuss how the student could carry out this comparison, and explain the trend expected in terms of the properties of the ions.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 equation, e.g. 2Mg(NO₃)₂ → 2MgO + 4NO₂ + O₂; brown gas (NO₂) observed\nIP2 heat equal moles (or equal masses) of each nitrate in identical boiling tubes with the same Bunsen flame / same distance\nIP3 measure the time taken for brown gas to appear (or for a glowing splint to relight) – a fair-test variable controlled; carry out in a fume cupboard because NO₂ is toxic\nIP4 thermal stability increases down the group, so barium nitrate takes longest to decompose\nIP5 cation radius increases down the group (charge stays 2+), so charge density decreases\nIP6 smaller cations polarise / distort the electron cloud of the nitrate ion more, weakening the N–O bonds so decomposition occurs more easily\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*Concentrated sulfuric acid is added separately to solid sodium chloride, solid sodium bromide and solid sodium iodide.\n\nDescribe the observations, identify the products and explain the trend in reducing power of the halide ions. Use equations and oxidation numbers in your answer.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 NaCl: steamy (misty) fumes of HCl; NaCl + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HCl; not redox (acid–base)\nIP2 NaBr: steamy fumes (HBr) and orange / brown fumes (Br₂) and a colourless choking gas (SO₂)\nIP3 2HBr + H₂SO₄ → Br₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O; sulfur reduced from +6 to +4\nIP4 NaI: purple vapour / black solid (I₂), yellow solid (S) and smell of bad eggs (H₂S)\nIP5 e.g. 8HI + H₂SO₄ → 4I₂ + H₂S + 4H₂O; sulfur reduced to +4, 0 and −2 (iodide reduces sulfur furthest)\nIP6 reducing power increases Cl⁻ to Br⁻ to I⁻ because ionic radius increases / more shielding, so the outer electron is lost more easily\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*A blue solution of copper(II) sulfate contains [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ ions. When concentrated hydrochloric acid is added, the solution turns yellow-green. When excess aqueous ammonia is added to a fresh sample, a pale blue precipitate forms and then dissolves to give a deep blue solution.\n\nExplain why copper(II) complexes are coloured and why the colour changes in these reactions. Include the formulae and shapes of the complexes formed.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 ligands split the 3d orbitals into two (sets of) energy levels\nIP2 an electron is promoted by absorbing a frequency of visible light (ΔE = hf); the complementary colour is seen\nIP3 with conc HCl: ligand exchange gives [CuCl₄]²⁻, tetrahedral, coordination number changes from 6 to 4\nIP4 with ammonia: pale blue precipitate Cu(OH)₂(H₂O)₄ then deep blue [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ (octahedral)\nIP5 a change of ligand changes the size of the energy gap ΔE between the d-orbitals\nIP6 so a different frequency of light is absorbed, giving a different colour; change in coordination number / shape also changes the splitting\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*Transition metals and their compounds act as catalysts.\n\nIn the Contact process, vanadium(V) oxide catalyses the reaction 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃.\nIn aqueous solution, iron(II) ions catalyse the reaction S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂.\n\nDiscuss how each of these catalysts works. Your answer should include equations and refer to the types of catalysis.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 V₂O₅ is a heterogeneous catalyst (different phase from the gaseous reactants); reactants adsorb onto the surface / active sites\nIP2 bonds in reactants are weakened, reaction occurs, products desorb; adsorption must be neither too strong nor too weak\nIP3 SO₂ + V₂O₅ → SO₃ + V₂O₄ and V₂O₄ + ½O₂ → V₂O₅; vanadium changes oxidation state between +5 and +4 and is regenerated\nIP4 Fe²⁺ is a homogeneous catalyst (same phase); uncatalysed reaction is slow because the two negative ions repel each other\nIP5 S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺ then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂\nIP6 each step involves oppositely charged ions, so the alternative route has lower activation energy; Fe²⁺ is regenerated (Fe³⁺ would also catalyse)\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` }
    ]
  },
  'T4': {
    name: 'Organic Chemistry I',
    questions: [
      { q: `A halogenoalkane used in the synthesis of a fragrance has the structural formula CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₂Br.\n\nGive the systematic name of this compound.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `1-bromo-3-methylbutane (1)\nIgnore missing hyphens or commas\nDo not award 4-bromo-2-methylbutane` },
      { q: `A student adds a few drops of bromine water to a sample of cyclohexene and shakes the mixture.\n\nState the colour change seen.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `orange / yellow / brown → colourless (1)\nBoth colours needed\nDo not award "clear" for colourless` },
      { q: `In a refinery, decane, C₁₀H₂₂, is cracked catalytically at about 450 °C and slightly above atmospheric pressure.\n\nIdentify the type of catalyst used in catalytic cracking.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `zeolite (1)\nAllow aluminosilicate / silica–alumina / aluminium oxide with silicon dioxide\nIgnore "heterogeneous" on its own` },
      { q: `The mass spectrum of propanone, CH₃COCH₃, has its base peak (tallest peak) at m/z = 43.\n\nIdentify the species responsible for the peak at m/z = 43.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `CH₃CO⁺ (1)\nAllow [C₂H₃O]⁺ or a displayed formula with a + charge\nPositive charge required\nDo not award C₃H₇⁺` },
      { q: `As part of a core practical on the oxidation of alcohols, a student warms 2-methylpropan-2-ol with sodium dichromate(VI) acidified with dilute sulfuric acid.\n\nState what the student would see.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `(solution) stays orange / no (colour) change (1)\nAllow "does not turn green"\nIgnore any reason given (tertiary alcohol not oxidised)` },
      { q: `In an investigation of the rates of hydrolysis of halogenoalkanes, a student adds 1-iodobutane to a mixture of ethanol and aqueous silver nitrate in a water bath at 50 °C. A precipitate slowly forms.\n\nState the colour of the precipitate.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `yellow (1)\nAllow pale yellow\nDo not award cream or white` },
      { q: `2-chloro-2-methylpropane is prepared by shaking 2-methylpropan-2-ol with concentrated hydrochloric acid. The organic layer is separated and then shaken with sodium hydrogencarbonate solution in a separating funnel.\n\nGive the reason for shaking the organic layer with sodium hydrogencarbonate solution.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `to neutralise / remove (traces of) hydrochloric acid / acid (1)\nAllow "to remove HCl"\nIgnore "to remove impurities" unqualified` },
      { q: `Some waste poly(chloroethene), PVC, is incinerated to generate electricity. Hydrogen chloride is one of the gases produced.\n\nSuggest how the hydrogen chloride is removed from the waste gases before they are released.`, marks: 1, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `pass the gases through / react with a base, e.g. calcium oxide / calcium carbonate / sodium hydroxide (solution) (1)\nAllow "scrubbing" with a named base or alkali\nIgnore "filters" or "scrubbers" with no base named` },
      { q: `Propan-1-ol is heated under reflux with excess acidified sodium dichromate(VI). The infrared spectrum of the purified organic product has a very broad absorption between 3300 and 2500 cm⁻¹ and a strong absorption at 1715 cm⁻¹.\n\nIdentify the organic product.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `propanoic acid / CH₃CH₂COOH (1)\nAllow "a carboxylic acid" only if propanoic acid is not given and a correct formula is\nDo not award propanal` },
      { q: `2-chlorobut-2-ene has the structural formula CH₃CH=C(Cl)CH₃.\n\nExplain why 2-chlorobut-2-ene exists as E and Z isomers.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(there is) restricted rotation about the C=C / double bond (1)\neach carbon atom of the C=C has two different groups / atoms attached (1)\nAllow "no rotation" for restricted rotation\nIgnore references to cis/trans` },
      { q: `One stereoisomer of 2-chlorobut-2-ene, CH₃CH=C(Cl)CH₃, has the chlorine atom on carbon-2 and the methyl group on carbon-3 on the same side of the double bond.\n\nDeduce whether this is the E or Z isomer. Justify your answer using the Cahn–Ingold–Prelog (CIP) priority rules.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `on carbon-2, Cl has higher priority than CH₃ (higher atomic number) AND on carbon-3, CH₃ has higher priority than H (1)\nthe two higher-priority groups are on the same side so it is the Z isomer (1)\nZ with no justification scores 0` },
      { q: `An alkane X has the molecular formula C₅H₁₂. When X reacts with chlorine in ultraviolet light, only one monochloro product, C₅H₁₁Cl, is formed.\n\nDeduce the structure of X and give its name.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `C(CH₃)₄ – structure showing a central carbon bonded to four CH₃ groups (1)\n2,2-dimethylpropane (1)\nAllow name mark only if structure is correct or missing\nAllow dimethylpropane` },
      { q: `1-bromopropane can be prepared in the laboratory from propan-1-ol without using hydrogen bromide gas directly.\n\nGive the reagents used to convert propan-1-ol into 1-bromopropane and the type of reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `potassium bromide and (50%) concentrated sulfuric acid (1)\nAllow sodium bromide for potassium bromide\nAllow phosphorus tribromide / PBr₃\nsubstitution (1)\nAllow nucleophilic substitution\nIgnore "halogenation"` },
      { q: `Heptane, C₇H₁₆, is reformed in a refinery to produce methylcyclohexane and hydrogen.\n\nWrite an equation for this reaction and state why reforming is carried out.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `C₇H₁₆ → C₇H₁₄ + H₂ (1)\nAllow skeletal or structural formula for methylcyclohexane\ncyclic / branched hydrocarbons burn more smoothly / efficiently in engines / reduce knocking / increase octane number (1)\nIgnore "to make better fuels" unqualified` },
      { q: `Decane, C₁₀H₂₂, is cracked to produce octane and ethene.\n\nWrite an equation for this reaction and give one reason why cracking is economically important.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄ (1)\nAllow structural formulae\nany one from (1):\n• demand for shorter-chain alkanes / petrol is greater than supply from fractional distillation\n• alkenes are produced which are used to make polymers / as chemical feedstock\nIgnore "makes more useful products" unqualified` },
      { q: `Butan-2-ol is heated under reflux with excess acidified potassium dichromate(VI). The infrared spectrum of the product has a strong absorption at 1715 cm⁻¹ and no absorption between 3750 and 3200 cm⁻¹.\n\nName the organic product and classify butan-2-ol as a primary, secondary or tertiary alcohol.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `butanone (1)\nAllow butan-2-one\nsecondary (1)\nMarks are independent` },
      { q: `A student investigates the rates of hydrolysis of 1-chlorobutane and 1-iodobutane using aqueous silver nitrate in ethanol at 50 °C. The C–Cl bond is more polar than the C–I bond, but 1-iodobutane is hydrolysed much faster.\n\nExplain why 1-iodobutane is hydrolysed faster than 1-chlorobutane.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `C–I bond (enthalpy) is weaker / lower than C–Cl bond (enthalpy) (1)\nless energy is needed to break the C–I bond so it breaks more easily / lower activation energy (1)\nAllow C–I bond is longer for first mark\nDo not award references to polarity as the reason` },
      { q: `In a core practical, ethanol is added dropwise to hot acidified sodium dichromate(VI), and the product ethanal (boiling temperature 21 °C) is distilled off immediately as it forms. Ethanol boils at 78 °C.\n\nExplain why the ethanal is distilled off as soon as it forms.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `ethanal has the lowest boiling temperature / is very volatile so it vaporises and leaves the reaction mixture (1)\nthis prevents further oxidation (of ethanal) to ethanoic acid (1)\nAllow "stops it reacting with the oxidising agent"` },
      { q: `A sample of an alcohol with molecular formula C₃H₈O is analysed by mass spectrometry. The base peak (tallest peak) is at m/z = 31.\n\nDeduce whether the alcohol is propan-1-ol or propan-2-ol. Justify your answer by identifying the fragment at m/z = 31.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `fragment is CH₂OH⁺ (1)\nCharge required\npropan-1-ol, since only a primary alcohol / CH₃CH₂CH₂OH has a CH₂OH group that can form this fragment (1)\nAllow propan-2-ol would give CH₃CHOH⁺ at m/z = 45` },
      { q: `Propene, CH₂=CHCH₃, is polymerised to make poly(propene), which is used for food containers.\n\nDraw the repeat unit of poly(propene).`, marks: 2, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `two carbon atoms joined by a single bond in the main chain, with one CH₃ group on one carbon and H atoms completing each carbon (1)\ncontinuation bonds through brackets / extending from each main-chain carbon (1)\nIgnore n\nDo not award if a C=C is shown` },
      { q: `Butan-2-ol is heated with concentrated phosphoric acid. A mixture of three isomeric alkenes with molecular formula C₄H₈ is collected.\n\nName the three alkenes formed.`, marks: 2, command: 'Name', markScheme: `but-1-ene (1)\nE-but-2-ene AND Z-but-2-ene (1)\nAllow trans- and cis-but-2-ene\nAll three correct = 2; two correct = 1` },
      { q: `Propene is shaken at room temperature with dilute potassium manganate(VII) acidified with dilute sulfuric acid. The organic product is propane-1,2-diol.\n\nState the colour change seen and give the type of reaction occurring.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `purple → colourless (1)\nBoth colours needed\noxidation (1)\nAllow "addition" for the reaction type` },
      { q: `Ethene reacts with bromine at room temperature to form 1,2-dibromoethane.\n\nDraw the mechanism for this reaction. Include curly arrows, and relevant dipoles and lone pairs.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `induced dipole on Br–Br (Brδ+ nearer the C=C, Brδ−) AND curly arrow from C=C to the δ+ Br (1)\ncurly arrow from Br–Br bond to the δ− Br (1)\ncarbocation intermediate CH₂⁺–CH₂Br AND curly arrow from a lone pair on Br⁻ to C⁺ (1)\nAllow a bromonium ion intermediate\nThe arrow must start at the bond / lone pair` },
      { q: `Methane reacts with excess chlorine in ultraviolet light. The product mixture contains chloromethane, CH₃Cl, but also dichloromethane, CH₂Cl₂, and other compounds.\n\nExplain, using equations for two propagation steps, how dichloromethane is formed.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `CH₃Cl + Cl• → CH₂Cl• + HCl (1)\nCH₂Cl• + Cl₂ → CH₂Cl₂ + Cl• (1)\nfurther / multiple substitution occurs because chlorine radicals collide with / remove H from the chloromethane already formed (1)\nDots on radicals required at least once; penalise missing dots once only` },
      { q: `Hydrogen bromide adds to propene to give two products. The major product is 2-bromopropane and the minor product is 1-bromopropane.\n\nExplain why 2-bromopropane is the major product.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `forming 2-bromopropane goes via a secondary carbocation, CH₃C⁺HCH₃, whereas 1-bromopropane goes via a primary carbocation (1)\nthe secondary carbocation is more stable because it has more alkyl groups which are electron-releasing / have a positive inductive effect (1)\nso the secondary carbocation is formed more readily / faster / lower activation energy (1)\nAllow "Markovnikov" only if explained in terms of carbocation stability` },
      { q: `A student prepared 2-chloro-2-methylpropane, C₄H₉Cl, by shaking 10.0 cm³ of 2-methylpropan-2-ol, C₄H₁₀O, with excess concentrated hydrochloric acid.\n(CH₃)₃COH + HCl → (CH₃)₃CCl + H₂O\nDensity of 2-methylpropan-2-ol = 0.775 g cm⁻³\nMass of pure product obtained = 6.20 g\nMolar masses / g mol⁻¹: C₄H₁₀O = 74.0; C₄H₉Cl = 92.5\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the percentage yield of 2-chloro-2-methylpropane. Give your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mass of alcohol = 10.0 × 0.775 = 7.75 g and moles = 7.75 ÷ 74.0 = 0.10473 mol (1)\ntheoretical mass of product = 0.10473 × 92.5 = 9.6875 g (1)\npercentage yield = 6.20 ÷ 9.6875 × 100 = 64.0 % (1)\nTE throughout\nAllow 64 %\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks` },
      { q: `A student oxidises propan-1-ol with acidified sodium dichromate(VI), distilling the product as it forms. The student claims that the distillate is pure propanal. The infrared spectrum of the distillate shows a strong absorption at 1730 cm⁻¹ and no absorption between 3750 and 2500 cm⁻¹.\nInfrared data:\nO–H (alcohols) 3750–3200 cm⁻¹\nO–H (carboxylic acids) 3300–2500 cm⁻¹\nC=O (aldehydes) 1740–1720 cm⁻¹\nC=O (carboxylic acids) 1725–1700 cm⁻¹\n\nExplain how the spectrum supports the student's claim.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `absorption at 1730 cm⁻¹ shows C=O (in the range for an aldehyde) (1)\nno absorption at 3750–3200 cm⁻¹ so no O–H of an alcohol / no unreacted propan-1-ol (1)\nno (broad) absorption at 3300–2500 cm⁻¹ so no O–H of a carboxylic acid / no propanoic acid (1)\nAllow "no peak around 3300" for both O–H marks if both alcohol and acid are named` },
      { q: `A compound has molecular formula C₃H₆O and is either propanal, CH₃CH₂CHO, or propanone, CH₃COCH₃. Its mass spectrum has a molecular ion peak at m/z = 58, a base peak at m/z = 43 and a peak at m/z = 15. There is no significant peak at m/z = 29.\n\nDeduce which compound this is. Justify your answer by identifying the species responsible for two of the peaks.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `m/z = 43 is CH₃CO⁺ (1)\nm/z = 15 is CH₃⁺ (1)\npropanone, because propanal would give a (strong) peak at m/z = 29 from CH₃CH₂⁺ / CHO⁺ and cannot form CH₃CO⁺ (1)\nPositive charges required; penalise once only\nAllow C₂H₃O⁺ for CH₃CO⁺` },
      { q: `A student compares the rates of hydrolysis of three bromoalkanes. 1 cm³ of ethanol is added to each of three test tubes, followed by 2 drops of a bromoalkane. The tubes are placed in a water bath at 50 °C. 1 cm³ of aqueous silver nitrate, also at 50 °C, is added to each tube and the time for a precipitate to appear is recorded.\n\nExplain why ethanol is added, why silver nitrate is used and why all the tubes are placed in the same water bath.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `ethanol is a (common) solvent so the bromoalkane and aqueous silver nitrate mix / dissolve (1)\nsilver ions react with the bromide ions produced to form a precipitate (of AgBr), so the time shows how fast hydrolysis happens (1)\nthe same temperature is needed because temperature affects the rate, so it is a fair test / control variable (1)\nAllow "halogenoalkanes are insoluble in water" for first mark` },
      { q: `2-bromopropane reacts with potassium hydroxide. The organic product depends on the conditions used.\n\nCompare the reactions of 2-bromopropane with potassium hydroxide under different conditions. Your answer should give the conditions, the organic products and the role of the hydroxide ion in each.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `aqueous (KOH), warm / heat under reflux → propan-2-ol (1)\nethanolic / alcoholic (KOH), heat under reflux → propene (1)\nOH⁻ acts as a nucleophile in substitution (aqueous) but as a base in elimination (ethanolic) (1)\nAllow names or formulae of products` },
      { q: `In the preparation of 2-chloro-2-methylpropane, the crude product is a mixture of an organic layer and an aqueous layer. The organic layer is less dense than water.\n\nDescribe how the student should obtain a dry organic product from this mixture using a separating funnel.`, marks: 3, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `pour mixture into separating funnel with the tap closed, stopper, invert and release the pressure / open the tap regularly (1)\nallow the layers to separate, run off and discard the lower aqueous layer, then collect the upper organic layer in a (dry) flask (1)\nadd anhydrous sodium sulfate / magnesium sulfate / calcium chloride and swirl until the liquid is clear, then decant / filter (1)\nIgnore references to distillation` },
      { q: `Bioethanol is made by fermenting sugars from crops such as sugar cane, and is blended with petrol as a fuel. It is sometimes described as "carbon neutral".\n\nExplain why bioethanol could be described as carbon neutral and why, in practice, it is not completely carbon neutral.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the plants / crops absorb carbon dioxide during photosynthesis while growing (1)\nthe same amount of carbon dioxide is released when the ethanol is fermented and burned (1)\nany one from (1):\n• fossil fuels are burned for farming machinery / fertiliser production / transport / distillation\n• land clearing / deforestation releases CO₂\nAllow equations for photosynthesis, fermentation and combustion` },
      { q: `0.290 g of a liquid organic compound containing only carbon, hydrogen and oxygen was burned completely in oxygen. It produced 0.660 g of carbon dioxide and 0.270 g of water. The molecular ion peak in its mass spectrum is at m/z = 58.\nMolar masses / g mol⁻¹: CO₂ = 44.0; H₂O = 18.0; C = 12.0; H = 1.0; O = 16.0\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nDetermine the molecular formula of the compound. You must show your working.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `mol C = 0.660 ÷ 44.0 = 0.0150 AND mol H = 2 × 0.270 ÷ 18.0 = 0.0300 (1)\nmass O = 0.290 − 0.180 − 0.0300 = 0.0800 g so mol O = 0.00500 (1)\nratio C : H : O = 3 : 6 : 1, empirical formula C₃H₆O (Mr = 58) so molecular formula C₃H₆O (1)\nTE on incorrect moles` },
      { q: `Hydrogen bromide reacts with propene to form 2-bromopropane as the major product.\n\nDraw the mechanism for the formation of 2-bromopropane. Include curly arrows, and relevant dipoles and lone pairs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `dipole on H–Br shown as Hδ+ Brδ− (1)\ncurly arrow from C=C to the H AND curly arrow from the H–Br bond to Br (1)\nsecondary carbocation CH₃C⁺HCH₃ (1)\ncurly arrow from a lone pair on Br⁻ to the C⁺ (1)\nArrows must start from a bond or a lone pair\nPrimary carbocation leading to 1-bromopropane: max 3` },
      { q: `1-bromopropane is heated in a sealed tube with excess ammonia dissolved in ethanol. The organic product is propylamine, CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂.\n\nDraw the mechanism for this reaction. Include curly arrows, and relevant dipoles and lone pairs.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `C–Br bond dipole shown Cδ+ Brδ− (1)\ncurly arrow from the lone pair on N of NH₃ to the δ+ carbon (1)\ncurly arrow from the C–Br bond to Br, giving Br⁻ (1)\nintermediate CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₃⁺ AND a second NH₃ removing H⁺ (curly arrow from N–H bond to N) to give CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ + NH₄⁺ (1)\nAllow lone pair on N to be shown as two dots` },
      { q: `As part of a core practical, a student prepares ethanoic acid by oxidising ethanol with acidified sodium dichromate(VI). Ethanol boils at 78 °C and ethanoic acid at 118 °C.\n\nDescribe how the student should carry out this preparation to obtain a sample of ethanoic acid. Include the observations expected.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `use excess acidified sodium dichromate(VI) / excess oxidising agent (1)\nheat under reflux (with anti-bumping granules) for a period of time (1)\nthen rearrange the apparatus for distillation and collect the fraction boiling at about 118 °C (1)\nthe mixture changes from orange to green (1)\nAllow potassium dichromate(VI)\nIgnore safety points` },
      { q: `Cyclohexene is prepared by heating cyclohexanol with concentrated phosphoric acid and distilling off the product.\nC₆H₁₁OH → C₆H₁₀ + H₂O\nA student starts with 10.0 g of cyclohexanol and obtains 5.60 g of pure cyclohexene.\nMolar masses / g mol⁻¹: cyclohexanol = 100.0; cyclohexene = 82.0\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the percentage yield and suggest two reasons why the yield is less than 100 %.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `moles cyclohexanol = 0.100 mol AND theoretical mass cyclohexene = 8.20 g (1)\npercentage yield = 5.60 ÷ 8.20 × 100 = 68.3 % (1)\nany two from (2):\n• product lost during transfer / left in the separating funnel / on the drying agent\n• reaction incomplete / some cyclohexanol does not react / distils over unreacted\n• side reactions (e.g. ether formation)\n• evaporation of the volatile product\nTE on incorrect theoretical mass\nAllow 68 %` },
      { q: `Compound A is an alcohol with molecular formula C₄H₁₀O. Information about A:\n• its mass spectrum has a molecular ion peak at m/z = 74 and a base peak at m/z = 45\n• when A is heated under reflux with acidified potassium dichromate(VI), the solution turns from orange to green\n• the infrared spectrum of the organic product has a strong absorption at 1715 cm⁻¹ and no absorption between 3750 and 2500 cm⁻¹\n\nDeduce the structure of A. Justify your answer using all the information given.`, marks: 4, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `A is oxidised so it is a primary or secondary alcohol / not tertiary (1)\nproduct has C=O but no O–H so it is a ketone, so A is a secondary alcohol (1)\npeak at 45 is CH₃CHOH⁺ / [C₂H₅O]⁺ formed by loss of C₂H₅ (1)\nA is butan-2-ol, CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃ (1)\nStructure mark can be awarded from name or formula` },
      { q: `1-bromo-2-chloroprop-1-ene has the structural formula BrCH=C(Cl)CH₃. It exists as two stereoisomers.\n\nDraw the two stereoisomers and label each as E or Z.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `isomer with Br and Cl on the same side of the C=C (H and CH₃ on the same side) (1)\nisomer with Br and Cl on opposite sides of the C=C (1)\nisomer with Br and Cl on the same side labelled Z (1)\nisomer with Br and Cl on opposite sides labelled E (1)\nBond angles around C=C should be about 120°; allow any clear 2D drawing\nAllow labels only if the structures are correct` },
      { q: `Ethane reacts with chlorine in the presence of ultraviolet light to form chloroethane. Small amounts of butane are also found in the product mixture.\n\nDescribe the mechanism of this reaction. Include equations for initiation, two propagation steps and the termination step that produces butane.`, marks: 5, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `initiation: Cl₂ → 2Cl• (1)\nUV light causes homolytic fission / breaks the Cl–Cl bond (1)\npropagation: C₂H₆ + Cl• → C₂H₅• + HCl (1)\npropagation: C₂H₅• + Cl₂ → C₂H₅Cl + Cl• (1)\ntermination: 2C₂H₅• → C₄H₁₀ (1)\nDots on radicals required; penalise missing dots once only\nAllow structural formulae` },
      { q: `A student prepares 2-chloro-2-methylpropane by shaking 8.00 cm³ of 2-methylpropan-2-ol with 25.0 cm³ of concentrated hydrochloric acid, concentration 11.0 mol dm⁻³.\n(CH₃)₃COH + HCl → (CH₃)₃CCl + H₂O\nDensity of 2-methylpropan-2-ol = 0.775 g cm⁻³\nMass of purified product = 5.15 g\nMolar masses / g mol⁻¹: (CH₃)₃COH = 74.0; (CH₃)₃CCl = 92.5\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nShow that the hydrochloric acid is in excess and calculate the percentage yield of 2-chloro-2-methylpropane. Give your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `mass of alcohol = 8.00 × 0.775 = 6.20 g (1)\nmoles of alcohol = 6.20 ÷ 74.0 = 0.0838 mol (1)\nmoles of HCl = 0.0250 × 11.0 = 0.275 mol, which is greater than 0.0838 so HCl is in excess (1:1 ratio) (1)\ntheoretical mass of product = 0.0838 × 92.5 = 7.75 g (1)\npercentage yield = 5.15 ÷ 7.75 × 100 = 66.5 % (to 3 s.f.) (1)\nTE throughout\nAllow 66.4 % from rounded values` },
      { q: `A student investigates the rates of hydrolysis of three halogenoalkanes using aqueous silver nitrate in ethanol at 50 °C. The results are shown.\nHalogenoalkane | time for precipitate to appear / s\n1-chlorobutane | 1450\n1-bromobutane | 240\n1-iodobutane | 35\nBond enthalpies / kJ mol⁻¹: C–Cl 346; C–Br 290; C–I 228\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate a relative rate, 1/time, for each halogenoalkane and explain the trend in rate using the data.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1-chlorobutane: 1/1450 = 6.90 × 10⁻⁴ s⁻¹ (1)\n1-bromobutane: 1/240 = 4.17 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹ AND 1-iodobutane: 1/35 = 2.86 × 10⁻² s⁻¹ (1)\nrate increases from chloro to bromo to iodo (1)\nbond enthalpy decreases from C–Cl to C–I so the C–X bond breaks more easily (1)\nbond enthalpy is more important than bond polarity, since C–Cl is the most polar but reacts slowest (1)\nAllow rates to 2 s.f. or more; units not required` },
      { q: `*Large quantities of addition polymers such as poly(ethene) and poly(chloroethene) are thrown away every year.\n\nDiscuss the methods used to dispose of waste addition polymers. Your answer should include the advantages and disadvantages of each method.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 landfill: addition polymers are non-biodegradable / unreactive (strong C–C and C–H bonds, non-polar) so persist for a very long time and use up land\nIP2 incineration releases energy that can be used to generate electricity / heat\nIP3 incineration produces CO₂ (greenhouse gas) and, from PVC, toxic HCl (and possibly dioxins); HCl removed by scrubbing with a base\nIP4 mechanical recycling: polymers sorted, melted and remoulded; saves crude oil / raw materials, but sorting is labour-intensive / costly\nIP5 feedstock recycling: polymers broken down (e.g. by heating) into monomers / small molecules used to make new polymers or chemicals\nIP6 biodegradable / photodegradable polymers developed as alternatives, but may need specific conditions to break down / can be more expensive\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*In a core practical, 2-chloro-2-methylpropane (boiling temperature 51 °C) is prepared from 2-methylpropan-2-ol (boiling temperature 83 °C) using concentrated hydrochloric acid at room temperature.\n(CH₃)₃COH + HCl → (CH₃)₃CCl + H₂O\n\nDescribe how a pure, dry sample of 2-chloro-2-methylpropane can be prepared and how its purity can be checked.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 shake 2-methylpropan-2-ol with (excess) concentrated hydrochloric acid in a separating funnel (in a fume cupboard), releasing the pressure regularly\nIP2 allow the layers to separate, discard the (lower) aqueous layer and keep the (upper) organic layer\nIP3 shake the organic layer with sodium hydrogencarbonate solution to remove acid, releasing the pressure as CO₂ is formed; then wash with water\nIP4 dry the organic layer with anhydrous sodium sulfate (or MgSO₄ / CaCl₂) until the liquid is clear, then decant / filter\nIP5 distil the dried liquid and collect the fraction boiling at about 50–52 °C\nIP6 check purity by measuring the boiling temperature: a sharp boiling temperature close to 51 °C indicates a pure product (or use IR to show no O–H absorption)\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*Crude oil is a mixture of alkanes. Fractions from crude oil are processed by cracking and reforming before use as fuels. Burning these fuels in car engines releases pollutants.\n\nDiscuss how cracking and reforming improve the usefulness of crude oil fractions, and explain how pollutants are formed in car engines and how their release is reduced.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 cracking breaks long-chain alkanes into shorter-chain alkanes and alkenes, using heat and / or a zeolite catalyst, e.g. C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄\nIP2 cracking matches supply to demand for petrol / shorter-chain fuels and produces alkenes as feedstock for polymers\nIP3 reforming converts straight-chain alkanes into branched or cyclic hydrocarbons (e.g. hexane → cyclohexane + H₂), which burn more efficiently / reduce knocking\nIP4 incomplete combustion produces carbon monoxide (toxic) and carbon particulates / soot (and unburnt hydrocarbons)\nIP5 high temperatures in the engine cause nitrogen and oxygen from air to react, N₂ + O₂ → 2NO; sulfur impurities form SO₂ (acid rain)\nIP6 catalytic converters (Pt / Rh / Pd) convert CO and NO into CO₂ and N₂, e.g. 2CO + 2NO → 2CO₂ + N₂; sulfur removed from fuels before use\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*Three unlabelled bottles each contain a different alcohol with molecular formula C₄H₁₀O: butan-1-ol, butan-2-ol and 2-methylpropan-2-ol.\nInfrared data: O–H (alcohols) 3750–3200 cm⁻¹; O–H (carboxylic acids) 3300–2500 cm⁻¹; C=O (aldehydes) 1740–1720 cm⁻¹; C=O (ketones) 1720–1700 cm⁻¹\n\nExplain how a combination of a chemical test, infrared spectroscopy and mass spectrometry could be used to identify each alcohol.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 phosphorus(V) chloride gives misty / steamy fumes of HCl with all three, confirming an O–H group (so this test alone does not distinguish them)\nIP2 warm each with acidified potassium dichromate(VI): butan-1-ol and butan-2-ol turn the solution from orange to green; 2-methylpropan-2-ol (tertiary) stays orange\nIP3 butan-1-ol (primary) heated under reflux with excess oxidant gives butanoic acid; butan-2-ol (secondary) gives butanone\nIP4 IR of the oxidation products: butanoic acid shows a broad O–H at 3300–2500 cm⁻¹ and C=O; butanone shows C=O at 1720–1700 cm⁻¹ with no O–H\nIP5 mass spectrum: butan-1-ol gives a peak at m/z = 31 (CH₂OH⁺); butan-2-ol gives a base peak at m/z = 45 (CH₃CHOH⁺)\nIP6 2-methylpropan-2-ol gives a peak at m/z = 59 from loss of CH₃ to form (CH₃)₂COH⁺; all three give the molecular ion at m/z = 74 (may be weak)\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` }
    ]
  },
  'T5': {
    name: 'Thermodynamics, Acids and Bases',
    questions: [
      { q: `In the nitration of benzene, concentrated nitric acid reacts with concentrated sulfuric acid.\n\nHNO₃ + H₂SO₄ → H₂NO₃⁺ + HSO₄⁻\n\nIdentify the species that acts as a Brønsted–Lowry base in this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `HNO₃ / nitric acid (1)\nAllow HSO₄⁻ only if the student clearly refers to the reverse reaction.\nDo not award H₂SO₄.` },
      { q: `Hydrochloric acid is a strong acid. A student prepares a solution of hydrochloric acid of concentration 0.0250 mol dm⁻³.\n\nCalculate the pH of this solution.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `pH = −log 0.0250 = 1.60 (1)\nAllow 1.6 or 1.602.\nIgnore additional significant figures.` },
      { q: `Methanoic acid, HCOOH, is a weak acid found in ant stings. It dissociates partially in water.\n\nHCOOH(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + HCOO⁻(aq)\n\nWrite the expression for the acid dissociation constant, Ka, of methanoic acid.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Ka = [H⁺(aq)][HCOO⁻(aq)] / [HCOOH(aq)] (1)\nSquare brackets must be used.\nAllow H₃O⁺ in place of H⁺.\nIgnore state symbols.\nDo not award [H⁺]² / [HCOOH] as the answer to this question.` },
      { q: `In a Core Practical to find the Ka of a weak acid, a student measures the pH of several solutions in turn using a pH meter.\n\nGive a reason why the electrode of the pH meter is rinsed with deionised water before it is placed in each new solution.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `to remove traces of the previous solution so that it does not contaminate / change the concentration (or pH) of the next solution (1)\nAllow so that the reading is due only to the new solution.\nIgnore 'to clean it' alone.` },
      { q: `A student determines the Ka of ethanoic acid by measuring the pH of a half-neutralised solution of the acid. The student is told to carry out all measurements at the same temperature.\n\nState why the temperature must be kept constant.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Ka (the equilibrium constant) changes with temperature (1)\nAllow the position of the dissociation equilibrium depends on temperature.\nAllow pH readings / Kw depend on temperature.\nIgnore 'to make it a fair test' alone.` },
      { q: `The lattice energy of magnesium oxide is −3791 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nWrite the equation, including state symbols, for the change that corresponds to the lattice energy of magnesium oxide.`, marks: 1, command: 'Write', markScheme: `Mg²⁺(g) + O²⁻(g) → MgO(s) (1)\nBoth species and state symbols needed.\nDo not award the reverse equation or equations starting from Mg(s) or O₂(g).` },
      { q: `When sodium chloride dissolves in water, the Na⁺ and Cl⁻ ions become hydrated.\n\nState which atom in a water molecule is closest to a chloride ion in the hydrated Cl⁻(aq) ion.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `a hydrogen atom (the δ+ H of the water molecule) (1)\nDo not award oxygen.\nIgnore any reference to hydrogen bonding.` },
      { q: `When solid ammonium nitrate dissolves in water, the temperature of the solution falls.\n\nNH₄NO₃(s) + aq → NH₄⁺(aq) + NO₃⁻(aq)\n\nState the sign of the entropy change of the surroundings, ΔSsurroundings, for this process.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `negative (1)\nAllow ΔSsurroundings is less than zero.\nIgnore reasoning; do not award 'positive' with any reasoning.` },
      { q: `A student measures the enthalpy change of solution of potassium nitrate by dissolving a weighed sample of the solid in 50.0 cm³ of deionised water in an expanded polystyrene cup and recording the temperature change. The student uses the mass of the water, not the mass of the solution, in the calculation.\n\nState one other assumption that the student makes when calculating the energy transferred.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `Any one from:\nthe specific heat capacity of the solution is the same as that of water (4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹) (1)\nthe density of the solution / water is 1.00 g cm⁻³ (1)\nno heat is gained from (or lost to) the surroundings / the cup (1)\nAll the solid dissolves (1)\nIgnore 'the reaction is complete'.` },
      { q: `Ethanoic acid reacts with water as shown.\n\nCH₃COOH + H₂O ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H₃O⁺\n\nIdentify the two conjugate acid–base pairs in this equilibrium.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `CH₃COOH (acid) and CH₃COO⁻ (base) (1)\nH₃O⁺ (acid) and H₂O (base) (1)\nAllow the pairs in either order provided they are correctly linked.\nIf acid/base labels are omitted, allow both marks for correct pairings.\nDo not award a pair that links CH₃COOH with H₃O⁺ or H₂O with CH₃COO⁻.` },
      { q: `Barium hydroxide, Ba(OH)₂, is a strong base that is fully dissociated in aqueous solution.\n\nKw = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶ at 298 K.\n\nCalculate the pH of a 0.0150 mol dm⁻³ solution of barium hydroxide at 298 K.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[OH⁻] = 2 × 0.0150 = 0.0300 mol dm⁻³ (1)\n[H⁺] = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 0.0300 = 3.33 × 10⁻¹³ mol dm⁻³ so pH = 12.48 (1)\nAllow via pOH = 1.52 and pH = 14 − 1.52.\nTE on incorrect [OH⁻]: 0.0150 used gives pH = 12.18 for 1 mark.\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks.` },
      { q: `The ionic product of water, Kw, is 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶ at 25 °C and 5.48 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶ at 50 °C.\n\nH₂O(l) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + OH⁻(aq)\n\nDeduce the sign of the enthalpy change for the ionisation of water. Justify your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `ΔH is positive / endothermic (1)\nKw increases with temperature so the equilibrium shifts to the right when heated, which is the endothermic direction (1)\nThe second mark is dependent on the first mark or a correct reference to the equilibrium shifting right.\nAllow 'an increase in temperature favours the endothermic reaction'.` },
      { q: `A student calculates the pH of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid using the expression\n\n[H⁺] = √(Ka × 0.100)\n\nGive two assumptions made in this calculation.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `[H⁺] = [CH₃COO⁻] / all H⁺ ions come from the acid; the ionisation of water is negligible (1)\nthe equilibrium concentration of CH₃COOH is equal to its initial concentration (0.100 mol dm⁻³) / the dissociation of the acid is negligible (1)\nIgnore 'the temperature is 298 K'.` },
      { q: `The table shows pKa values for two carboxylic acids at 298 K.\n\nacid | pKa\nethanoic acid, CH₃COOH | 4.76\nchloroethanoic acid, CH₂ClCOOH | 2.86\n\nDeduce which acid is the stronger acid and calculate how many times greater its Ka value is than the Ka of the other acid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `chloroethanoic acid (because it has the lower pKa / larger Ka) (1)\nKa ratio = 10^(4.76 − 2.86) = 10^1.90 = 79 (times) (1)\nAllow 79.4 or 80.\nAllow via Ka values 1.38 × 10⁻³ and 1.74 × 10⁻⁵.` },
      { q: `The table gives the pH ranges of three indicators.\n\nindicator | pH range\nmethyl orange | 3.1–4.4\nbromothymol blue | 6.0–7.6\nphenolphthalein | 8.3–10.0\n\nA student titrates 25.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid with 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide solution.\n\nIdentify the most suitable indicator for this titration. Justify your choice.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `phenolphthalein (1)\nits pH range lies within the vertical (steep) section of the pH curve for a weak acid–strong base titration, which is roughly pH 7 to pH 11 / the equivalence point is above pH 7 (1)\nThe second mark is dependent on phenolphthalein.\nDo not award 'it changes colour at pH 7'.` },
      { q: `In a Core Practical, a student uses a pH meter to follow the pH of a solution of a weak acid as sodium hydroxide solution is added from a burette.\n\nDescribe how the pH meter should be calibrated before use.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `place the electrode in a buffer solution of known pH (e.g. pH 7.00) and adjust the meter to read that value (1)\nrinse the electrode with deionised water and repeat with a second buffer of different known pH (e.g. pH 4.00 or 10.00) (1)\nAllow the use of at least two buffer solutions of known pH for both marks only if adjustment of the reading is described.\nIgnore 'use distilled water to calibrate'.` },
      { q: `In a titration to find the Ka of a weak acid, the burette used has an uncertainty of ±0.05 cm³ for each reading. The initial reading was 0.00 cm³ and the final reading was 24.90 cm³.\n\nCalculate the percentage uncertainty in the titre.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `total uncertainty = 2 × 0.05 = 0.10 cm³ (1)\npercentage uncertainty = 0.10 / 24.90 × 100 = 0.40 % (1)\nAllow 0.402 % or 0.4 %.\nTE on 0.05 cm³ used: 0.20 % scores 1 mark.` },
      { q: `The standard enthalpy change of neutralisation of hydrochloric acid by sodium hydroxide solution is −57.1 kJ mol⁻¹. For ethanoic acid with sodium hydroxide solution the value is −55.2 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nExplain why the value for ethanoic acid is less exothermic.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `ethanoic acid is a weak acid / only partially dissociated, so it must dissociate (ionise) during the neutralisation (1)\ndissociation (breaking the O–H bond) is endothermic / requires energy, which reduces the overall energy released (1)\nDo not award 'ethanoic acid has fewer H⁺ ions so less heat is released' without reference to energy needed for dissociation.` },
      { q: `Magnesium oxide, MgO, and sodium fluoride, NaF, have the same crystal structure. The ions in both compounds are isoelectronic.\n\nlattice energy of MgO = −3791 kJ mol⁻¹\nlattice energy of NaF = −918 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nExplain why the lattice energy of magnesium oxide is much more exothermic than that of sodium fluoride.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Mg²⁺ and O²⁻ have double the charge of Na⁺ and F⁻ / ions in MgO have greater charges (and Mg²⁺ is smaller than Na⁺) (1)\nso there is stronger electrostatic attraction between the oppositely charged ions in MgO (1)\nAllow greater charge density of ions for the first mark.\nDo not award any reference to molecules, intermolecular forces or covalent bonds.` },
      { q: `The table shows enthalpy changes of hydration for two Group 2 ions.\n\nion | ΔhydH / kJ mol⁻¹\nMg²⁺ | −1920\nCa²⁺ | −1579\n\nExplain why the enthalpy change of hydration of Mg²⁺ is more exothermic than that of Ca²⁺.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Mg²⁺ has a smaller ionic radius (same charge) / higher charge density (1)\nso there is stronger attraction between the ion and the δ− oxygen atoms of (the lone pairs on) water molecules (1)\nDo not award 'Mg²⁺ forms stronger hydrogen bonds with water'.` },
      { q: `Two reactions are shown.\n\nReaction 1: CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g)\nReaction 2: 2NO₂(g) → N₂O₄(g)\n\nPredict the sign of the entropy change of the system, ΔSsystem, for each reaction. Justify your answers.`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `Reaction 1: positive, because a gas is formed from a solid / number of moles of gas increases from 0 to 1 (1)\nReaction 2: negative, because the number of moles of gas decreases from 2 to 1 (so fewer ways of arranging the particles and energy) (1)\nSign and reason needed for each mark.` },
      { q: `A student carries out the Core Practical to measure the enthalpy change of solution of potassium nitrate. The student reads the thermometer once just before adding the solid and once when the temperature appears to stop falling.\n\nSuggest how the student could improve the method to obtain a more accurate value for the temperature change. Justify your suggestion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `record the temperature at regular intervals (e.g. every 30 s) before and after adding the solid, plot a temperature–time graph and extrapolate the cooling line back to the time of mixing (1)\nthis compensates for heat gained from the surroundings while the solid is dissolving (so the true maximum temperature change is found) (1)\nAllow insulating the cup / adding a lid for 1 mark only, with justification that it reduces heat gained from the surroundings.` },
      { q: `At 50 °C, the ionic product of water, Kw, is 5.48 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶.\n\nCalculate the pH of pure water at 50 °C and explain why the water is still neutral.\n\nGive your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[H⁺] = √(5.48 × 10⁻¹⁴) = 2.34 × 10⁻⁷ mol dm⁻³ (1)\npH = −log(2.34 × 10⁻⁷) = 6.63 (1)\nneutral because [H⁺] = [OH⁻] (1)\nDo not award the third mark for 'pH 7 is neutral' or for any answer implying pure water becomes acidic.\nCorrect pH with no working scores the first two marks.` },
      { q: `Propanoic acid, CH₃CH₂COOH, is a weak acid. Ka = 1.35 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ at 298 K.\n\nCalculate the pH of a 0.100 mol dm⁻³ solution of propanoic acid at 298 K.\n\nGive your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Ka = [H⁺]² / [CH₃CH₂COOH] or [H⁺]² = 1.35 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.100 (1)\n[H⁺] = √(1.35 × 10⁻⁶) = 1.16 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ (1)\npH = 2.93 (1)\nTE on incorrect [H⁺].\nAllow 2.9.\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks.` },
      { q: `A student made a 0.0500 mol dm⁻³ solution of methanoic acid, HCOOH, and measured its pH using a calibrated pH meter. The pH was 2.53.\n\nCalculate the value of Ka for methanoic acid. Include units in your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[H⁺] = 10⁻²·⁵³ = 2.95 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ (1)\nKa = (2.95 × 10⁻³)² / 0.0500 = 1.74 × 10⁻⁴ (1)\nunits: mol dm⁻³ (1)\nAllow 1.7 × 10⁻⁴.\nAllow 1.85 × 10⁻⁴ if the student subtracts [H⁺] from 0.0500 in the denominator.\nTE on incorrect [H⁺] for the second mark.\nThe units mark is independent.` },
      { q: `A buffer solution is made by mixing ethanoic acid and sodium ethanoate. In the buffer, the concentration of ethanoic acid is 0.200 mol dm⁻³ and the concentration of sodium ethanoate is 0.150 mol dm⁻³.\n\nKa for ethanoic acid = 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ at 298 K.\n\nCalculate the pH of this buffer solution at 298 K.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[H⁺] = Ka × [CH₃COOH] / [CH₃COO⁻] (1)\n[H⁺] = 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.200 / 0.150 = 2.32 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ (1)\npH = 4.63 (1)\nAllow pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid]) = 4.76 + log(0.150/0.200) for the first two marks.\nTE on inverted ratio: [H⁺] = 1.31 × 10⁻⁵, pH = 4.88 scores 2 marks.\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks.` },
      { q: `In the Core Practical to find Ka of a weak monobasic acid, HA, a student added 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide solution from a burette to 25.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ HA and recorded the pH. (data written for this practice question)\n\nvolume of NaOH / cm³ | 0.0 | 5.0 | 10.0 | 12.5 | 15.0 | 20.0 | 24.0 | 25.0 | 26.0 | 30.0\npH | 2.43 | 3.25 | 3.67 | 3.85 | 4.03 | 4.45 | 5.23 | 8.27 | 11.29 | 11.96\n\nUse the data to determine the value of Ka for HA. Show your working.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `volume at equivalence (end) point = 25.0 cm³ so half-neutralisation is at 12.5 cm³ (1)\nat half-neutralisation [HA] = [A⁻] so pH = pKa = 3.85 (1)\nKa = 10⁻³·⁸⁵ = 1.41 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³ (1)\nAllow 1.4 × 10⁻⁴.\nAllow full marks for using the initial pH: [H⁺] = 10⁻²·⁴³, Ka = (3.72 × 10⁻³)² / 0.100 = 1.38 × 10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³, provided the method is clearly shown.\nIgnore missing units.` },
      { q: `The table shows experimental lattice energies (from Born–Haber cycles) and theoretical lattice energies (calculated using a perfect ionic model) for two compounds. (data written for this practice question)\n\ncompound | experimental / kJ mol⁻¹ | theoretical / kJ mol⁻¹\nNaCl | −780 | −770\nAgCl | −905 | −833\n\nExplain the difference between the experimental and theoretical values for silver chloride.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the experimental value is more exothermic than the theoretical value, so the bonding is not purely ionic / has some covalent character (1)\nthe Ag⁺ ion polarises / distorts the electron cloud of the Cl⁻ ion (1)\ncausing electron density to be shared between the ions / extra attraction not accounted for by the ionic model (which assumes spherical ions and point charges) (1)\nAllow the reverse argument comparing with NaCl, which is almost completely ionic.\nDo not award 'AgCl is covalent' without qualification.` },
      { q: `Magnesium iodide, MgI₂, and sodium fluoride, NaF, are both described as ionic compounds.\n\nDeduce which compound has the greater degree of covalent character. Explain your answer in terms of the ions present.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `MgI₂ (1)\nMg²⁺ has a higher charge density than Na⁺ (it is smaller and has a 2+ charge) so it is more polarising (1)\nI⁻ is larger than F⁻ so its electron cloud is more easily distorted / it is more polarisable (1)\nThe explanation marks can be awarded even if MgI₂ is not named, provided the reasoning is correct.\nDo not award reference to electronegativity difference alone.` },
      { q: `The enthalpy change of solution of calcium chloride can be calculated from the following data.\n\nlattice energy of CaCl₂ = −2258 kJ mol⁻¹\nenthalpy change of hydration of Ca²⁺ = −1579 kJ mol⁻¹\nenthalpy change of hydration of Cl⁻ = −378 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of solution of calcium chloride.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔsolH = −(lattice energy) + Σ ΔhydH (or a correct cycle drawn) (1)\nsum of hydration enthalpies = −1579 + 2(−378) = −2335 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\nΔsolH = +2258 − 2335 = −77 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\nSign required for the final mark.\nTE on failure to double the Cl⁻ value: +301 kJ mol⁻¹ scores 1 mark.\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks.` },
      { q: `The following data relate to sodium chloride.\n\nlattice energy of NaCl = −787 kJ mol⁻¹\nenthalpy change of hydration of Na⁺ = −406 kJ mol⁻¹\nenthalpy change of hydration of Cl⁻ = −378 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nDraw a labelled enthalpy level diagram for the dissolving of sodium chloride in water. Show the value of the enthalpy change of solution on your diagram.`, marks: 3, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Na⁺(g) + Cl⁻(g) at the top level, with NaCl(s) below it, linked by an arrow labelled lattice energy −787 kJ mol⁻¹ (arrow down) or +787 kJ mol⁻¹ (arrow up) (1)\nhydration step(s) from Na⁺(g) + Cl⁻(g) down to Na⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq), labelled −406 and −378 kJ mol⁻¹ (or −784 kJ mol⁻¹ in total) (1)\nNa⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) shown slightly above NaCl(s) with ΔsolH = +3 kJ mol⁻¹ labelled (1)\nState symbols needed on all species for full marks; penalise missing state symbols once.\nAllow a Hess cycle in place of an enthalpy level diagram for the first two marks only.` },
      { q: `Calcium carbonate decomposes on heating.\n\nCaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g)   ΔH = +178 kJ mol⁻¹   ΔSsystem = +161 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹\n\nCalculate the minimum temperature at which this reaction becomes thermodynamically feasible.\n\nGive your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `reaction is feasible when ΔG ≤ 0, so at the minimum temperature ΔH = TΔS / T = ΔH / ΔS (1)\nconversion of units: 178 000 J mol⁻¹ or 0.161 kJ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ (1)\nT = 178 000 / 161 = 1110 K (1)\nAllow 1106 K or 1105.6 K; allow 833 °C.\nTE on failure to convert units: 1.11 K scores 1 mark (first mark only).\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks.` },
      { q: `The Gibbs energy change for a reversible reaction at 298 K is −33.0 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nΔG = −RT ln K   R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹\n\nCalculate the value of the equilibrium constant, K, for this reaction at 298 K.\n\nGive your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ln K = −ΔG / RT = 33 000 / (8.31 × 298) (1)\nln K = 13.3 (1)\nK = e¹³·³ = 6.1 × 10⁵ (1)\nAllow 6.13 × 10⁵. Allow answers in the range 5.9 × 10⁵ to 6.2 × 10⁵ from rounding of ln K.\nTE on use of 33.0 instead of 33 000: ln K = 0.0133, K = 1.01 scores 1 mark.\nIgnore units; K has no units in this expression.` },
      { q: `A student added 40.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide solution to 25.0 cm³ of 0.120 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid.\n\nKw = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶ at 298 K.\n\nCalculate the pH of the resulting solution at 298 K.\n\nGive your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `moles of NaOH = 0.0400 × 0.100 = 4.00 × 10⁻³ and moles of HCl = 0.0250 × 0.120 = 3.00 × 10⁻³ (1)\nexcess OH⁻ = 1.00 × 10⁻³ mol (1)\n[OH⁻] = 1.00 × 10⁻³ / 0.0650 = 0.0154 mol dm⁻³ (1)\npH = 14.00 − 1.81 = 12.19 (1)\nAllow [H⁺] = 1.00 × 10⁻¹⁴ / 0.0154 = 6.50 × 10⁻¹³ for the final step.\nTE throughout; using 40.0 cm³ as the total volume gives pH 12.40 and scores 3 marks.\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks.` },
      { q: `A student wants to make a buffer solution of pH 5.00 by dissolving solid sodium ethanoate, CH₃COONa, in 250 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid. Assume the volume of the solution does not change.\n\nKa for ethanoic acid = 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³   Mr of CH₃COONa = 82.0\n\nCalculate the mass of sodium ethanoate needed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `[H⁺] = 10⁻⁵·⁰⁰ = 1.00 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ (1)\n[CH₃COO⁻] = Ka × [CH₃COOH] / [H⁺] = 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.100 / 1.00 × 10⁻⁵ = 0.174 mol dm⁻³ (1)\nmoles of CH₃COONa = 0.174 × 0.250 = 0.0435 mol (1)\nmass = 0.0435 × 82.0 = 3.57 g (1)\nTE throughout.\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks.` },
      { q: `A student adds 0.100 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide solution from a burette to 25.0 cm³ of 0.100 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid until a total of 40.0 cm³ has been added. The pKa of ethanoic acid is 4.76.\n\nSketch the pH curve for this titration. Label the axes and show the approximate pH values at the start, at half-neutralisation, at the equivalence point and at the end.`, marks: 4, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `axes labelled pH (vertical, 0–14) and volume of NaOH added / cm³ (horizontal) (1)\ncurve starts at about pH 2.5–3 and rises gently through a buffer region, passing through pH 4.76 (= pKa) at 12.5 cm³ (1)\nsteep (near-vertical) section at 25.0 cm³, from about pH 7 to pH 11, with equivalence point above 7 (about 8.5–9) (1)\ncurve levels off at about pH 12–13 by 40.0 cm³ (1)\nDo not award the third mark if the vertical section is not at 25.0 cm³.` },
      { q: `The table shows data for a Born–Haber cycle for magnesium chloride, MgCl₂.\n\nprocess | ΔH / kJ mol⁻¹\nenthalpy change of formation of MgCl₂(s) | −641\nenthalpy change of atomisation of magnesium | +148\nfirst ionisation energy of magnesium | +738\nsecond ionisation energy of magnesium | +1451\nenthalpy change of atomisation of chlorine (per mole of Cl atoms) | +121.7\nelectron affinity of chlorine | −349\n\nCalculate the lattice energy of magnesium chloride.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `atomisation of chlorine doubled: 2 × 121.7 = +243.4 (1)\nelectron affinity doubled: 2 × (−349) = −698 (1)\nlattice energy = −641 − (148 + 738 + 1451 + 243.4 − 698) (1)\n= −2523 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\nAllow −2520 kJ mol⁻¹.\nSign required for the final mark.\nTE on failure to double one value (−2402 or −2872) for 3 marks; failure to double both, −2751, scores 2 marks.\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks.` },
      { q: `Ammonia is manufactured by the Haber process.\n\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)   ΔH = −92.2 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nsubstance | standard molar entropy, S⦵ / J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹\nN₂(g) | 191.6\nH₂(g) | 130.6\nNH₃(g) | 192.3\n\nCalculate the total entropy change, ΔStotal, for this reaction at 298 K and use your answer to state whether the reaction is thermodynamically feasible at this temperature.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔSsystem = 2(192.3) − [191.6 + 3(130.6)] = −198.8 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ (1)\nΔSsurroundings = −ΔH / T = −(−92 200) / 298 (1)\n= +309.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ (1)\nΔStotal = +110.6 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, which is positive so the reaction is feasible (1)\nAllow +111 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.\nTE on incorrect ΔSsystem or ΔSsurroundings for the final mark provided the feasibility conclusion matches the sign.\nPenalise failure to convert kJ to J once only.` },
      { q: `A student carried out the Core Practical to determine the enthalpy change of solution of potassium nitrate, KNO₃.\n\nmass of KNO₃ added = 5.05 g\nvolume of deionised water = 50.0 cm³\ninitial temperature = 21.3 °C\nlowest temperature reached = 13.6 °C\n\nspecific heat capacity of water = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹   Mr of KNO₃ = 101.1\n\nCalculate the enthalpy change of solution of potassium nitrate in kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nGive your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `energy transferred = 50.0 × 4.18 × 7.7 = 1609 J (1)\nmoles of KNO₃ = 5.05 / 101.1 = 0.0500 mol (1)\nΔsolH = 1.609 / 0.0500 = +32.2 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\npositive sign (endothermic) and answer given to 2 or 3 significant figures (1)\nAllow +32 kJ mol⁻¹.\nTE on incorrect energy or moles.\nDo not award the final mark if the sign is negative.\nIgnore use of 55.05 g for the mass heated: gives +35.5 kJ mol⁻¹ and scores 3 marks.` },
      { q: `A buffer solution contains 0.500 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid and 0.500 mol dm⁻³ sodium ethanoate. Ka for ethanoic acid = 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³.\n\nA student adds 10.0 cm³ of 1.00 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide solution to 100 cm³ of this buffer.\n\nCalculate the pH of the buffer before and after the addition of the sodium hydroxide, and hence the change in pH.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `pH before = pKa (since [acid] = [salt]) = −log(1.74 × 10⁻⁵) = 4.76 (1)\nmoles of NaOH = 0.0100 mol; initial moles of CH₃COOH and CH₃COO⁻ = 0.0500 mol each (1)\nafter addition: CH₃COOH = 0.0400 mol and CH₃COO⁻ = 0.0600 mol (1)\n[H⁺] = 1.74 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.0400 / 0.0600 = 1.16 × 10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ (1)\npH after = 4.94, so the pH increases by 0.18 (1)\nAllow moles in place of concentrations in the ratio because the volume is the same for both.\nTE throughout.\nAllow an increase of 0.17–0.18.` },
      { q: `The second electron affinity of oxygen cannot be measured directly. It can be calculated using a Born–Haber cycle for calcium oxide.\n\nprocess | ΔH / kJ mol⁻¹\nenthalpy change of formation of CaO(s) | −635\nenthalpy change of atomisation of calcium | +193\nfirst ionisation energy of calcium | +590\nsecond ionisation energy of calcium | +1145\nenthalpy change of atomisation of oxygen | +249\nfirst electron affinity of oxygen | −141\nlattice energy of CaO(s) | −3464\n\nCalculate the second electron affinity of oxygen and explain the sign of your answer.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `correct Hess expression, e.g. ΔfH = ΔatH(Ca) + IE1 + IE2 + ΔatH(O) + EA1 + EA2 + LE (1)\nsum of known steps other than lattice energy and EA2 = 193 + 590 + 1145 + 249 − 141 = +2036 (1)\nEA2 = −635 − 2036 + 3464 (1)\nEA2 = +793 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\npositive / endothermic because the electron is added to a negative ion, O⁻, so energy is needed to overcome the repulsion between O⁻ and the electron (1)\nSign required for the fourth mark.\nTE on arithmetic errors for the fourth mark.\nThe fifth mark is independent of the calculation.` },
      { q: `Magnesium carbonate decomposes on heating.\n\nMgCO₃(s) → MgO(s) + CO₂(g)\n\nsubstance | ΔfH⦵ / kJ mol⁻¹ | S⦵ / J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹\nMgCO₃(s) | −1095.8 | 65.7\nMgO(s) | −601.7 | 26.9\nCO₂(g) | −393.5 | 213.6\n\nCalculate the minimum temperature, in K, at which the decomposition of magnesium carbonate becomes thermodynamically feasible.\n\nGive your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `ΔH = (−601.7 + −393.5) − (−1095.8) = +100.6 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\nΔSsystem = (26.9 + 213.6) − 65.7 = +174.8 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ (1)\nfeasible when ΔG = ΔH − TΔS ≤ 0, so minimum T = ΔH / ΔSsystem (1)\nT = 100 600 / 174.8 (correct unit conversion) (1)\n= 576 K (1)\nAllow 575.5 K.\nTE throughout; failure to convert units scores a maximum of 4 marks.\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks.` },
      { q: `*The pH of blood is kept close to 7.4 by several buffer systems. One of the most important is the carbonic acid–hydrogencarbonate buffer.\n\nH₂CO₃(aq) ⇌ H⁺(aq) + HCO₃⁻(aq)\n\nExplain how this buffer system keeps the pH of blood almost constant when small amounts of acid or alkali enter the blood, and explain why the pH would change if large amounts of acid were added.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 a buffer resists changes in pH when small amounts of acid or alkali are added\nIP2 the buffer contains relatively large concentrations (reservoirs) of both the weak acid H₂CO₃ and its conjugate base HCO₃⁻\nIP3 added H⁺ ions react with HCO₃⁻ to form H₂CO₃ / the equilibrium shifts to the left, removing most of the added H⁺\nIP4 added OH⁻ ions react with H⁺ (to form water) and more H₂CO₃ dissociates / equilibrium shifts to the right to replace the H⁺ (or OH⁻ + H₂CO₃ → HCO₃⁻ + H₂O)\nIP5 the ratio [H₂CO₃] / [HCO₃⁻] changes only slightly, so [H⁺] = Ka[H₂CO₃]/[HCO₃⁻] and the pH change very little\nIP6 if large amounts of acid are added, the HCO₃⁻ is used up, so the ratio changes significantly / the buffer capacity is exceeded and the pH falls\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*In a Core Practical, a student is asked to determine the acid dissociation constant, Ka, of ethanoic acid using the half-neutralisation method. The student is provided with approximately 0.1 mol dm⁻³ ethanoic acid, 0.1 mol dm⁻³ sodium hydroxide solution, phenolphthalein indicator, a calibrated pH meter and standard laboratory glassware.\n\nDescribe how the student should carry out this experiment and explain how the value of Ka is obtained from the results.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 use a pipette to transfer 25.0 cm³ of ethanoic acid to a conical flask and add a few drops of phenolphthalein\nIP2 titrate with sodium hydroxide solution from a burette until the solution just turns (permanently) pink and record the titre; repeat to obtain concordant titres\nIP3 pipette another 25.0 cm³ of the acid and add exactly half the mean titre of sodium hydroxide (without indicator) / or add the original 25.0 cm³ of acid to the neutralised solution so the acid is half-neutralised\nIP4 mix thoroughly and measure the pH of the half-neutralised solution with the calibrated pH meter, rinsing the electrode with deionised water first, at a constant (recorded) temperature\nIP5 at half-neutralisation [CH₃COOH] = [CH₃COO⁻], so Ka = [H⁺] / pH = pKa\nIP6 Ka = 10⁻ᵖᴴ, given in mol dm⁻³; repeat the pH measurement and take a mean\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*The table shows experimental lattice energies (from Born–Haber cycles) and theoretical lattice energies (from a perfect ionic model) for four compounds. (data written for this practice question)\n\ncompound | experimental / kJ mol⁻¹ | theoretical / kJ mol⁻¹\nNaF | −918 | −912\nNaI | −705 | −686\nMgF₂ | −2957 | −2913\nMgI₂ | −2327 | −1944\n\nDiscuss what the data show about the bonding in these compounds. In your answer, explain the factors that affect the magnitude of lattice energies and the differences between the experimental and theoretical values.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 lattice energy becomes more exothermic as ionic charge increases, e.g. Mg²⁺ compounds have much more exothermic values than Na⁺ compounds\nIP2 lattice energy becomes more exothermic as ionic radius decreases, e.g. fluorides are more exothermic than iodides, because the ions can get closer so the electrostatic attraction is stronger\nIP3 the theoretical model assumes ions are perfect spheres with point charges / the bonding is 100 % ionic\nIP4 NaF experimental and theoretical values are very close, so NaF is almost purely ionic\nIP5 the difference is largest for MgI₂ (about 380 kJ mol⁻¹), showing significant covalent character\nIP6 because the small, highly charged Mg²⁺ ion (high charge density) strongly polarises the large, easily distorted I⁻ ion, giving electron density between the ions\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*The Haber process for making ammonia is shown.\n\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g)   ΔH = −92.2 kJ mol⁻¹   ΔSsystem = −198.8 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹\n\nAt 298 K the reaction is thermodynamically feasible, but in industry it is carried out at about 700 K with an iron catalyst.\n\nDiscuss how the thermodynamic feasibility and the equilibrium constant of this reaction change with temperature, and explain why the reaction does not take place at a noticeable rate at room temperature even though it is feasible.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 ΔStotal = ΔSsystem + ΔSsurroundings, where ΔSsurroundings = −ΔH/T is positive (+309 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ at 298 K) and ΔSsystem is negative because the moles of gas decrease from 4 to 2\nIP2 as temperature increases, ΔSsurroundings becomes less positive (e.g. +132 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ at 700 K), so ΔStotal becomes less positive / negative (at 700 K about −67 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)\nIP3 equivalently ΔG = ΔH − TΔS becomes less negative / positive as T increases; the reaction stops being feasible above about 464 K (ΔH/ΔS)\nIP4 since ΔStotal = R ln K (or ΔG = −RT ln K), K decreases as the temperature increases, so the equilibrium yield of ammonia falls\nIP5 at room temperature the reaction is kinetically inert / the activation energy is very high (the N≡N bond is very strong), so very few collisions have energy ≥ Ea and the rate is negligible\nIP6 a higher temperature and a catalyst (which provides an alternative route of lower Ea) are used to obtain an acceptable rate, a compromise with yield; feasibility gives no information about rate\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` }
    ]
  },
  'T6': {
    name: 'Advanced Organic Chemistry and Analysis',
    questions: [
      { q: `2-Hydroxypropanoic acid (lactic acid), CH₃CH(OH)COOH, extracted from sour milk rotates the plane of plane-polarised light.\nA sample made in the laboratory by reacting ethanal with HCN, followed by hydrolysis of the nitrile with dilute acid, has no effect on plane-polarised light.\n\nState the term used to describe the laboratory sample.`, marks: 1, command: 'State', markScheme: `racemic mixture / racemate (1)\nAllow: equimolar mixture of the two enantiomers / optical isomers\nDo not award: "optically inactive" alone` },
      { q: `Benzene reacts with bromine at room temperature only when a halogen carrier is present. The product is bromobenzene.\n\nIdentify a suitable halogen carrier for this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `iron(III) bromide / FeBr₃ (1)\nAllow: aluminium bromide / AlBr₃ / iron filings / Fe\nIgnore: "catalyst" alone` },
      { q: `Compound X has the molecular formula C₄H₈O.\nX forms an orange precipitate with 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine.\nX gives no change when warmed with Fehling's solution.\n\nGive the name of compound X.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `butanone (1)\nAllow: butan-2-one\nDo not award: butanal / 2-methylpropanal` },
      { q: `A student adds solid phosphorus(V) chloride, PCl₅, to a small volume of dry ethanoic acid in a fume cupboard. Steamy fumes of hydrogen chloride are produced.\n\nGive the name of the organic product of this reaction.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `ethanoyl chloride (1)\nAllow: CH₃COCl\nIgnore: POCl₃ / HCl given as additional products` },
      { q: `The ¹H NMR spectrum of an organic liquid is recorded with the sample dissolved in CDCl₃ rather than in CHCl₃. A small amount of tetramethylsilane (TMS) is also added.\n\nGive a reason why CDCl₃ is used as the solvent rather than CHCl₃.`, marks: 1, command: 'Give', markScheme: `CDCl₃ contains no ¹H atoms so the solvent gives no peak / does not interfere with the spectrum (1)\nAllow: deuterium does not absorb in the same region as ¹H\nIgnore: references to TMS` },
      { q: `A student carries out thin-layer chromatography on a sample of aspirin.\nThe solvent front moves 8.0 cm from the base line. The aspirin spot moves 3.6 cm from the base line.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nCalculate the Rf value of aspirin.`, marks: 1, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Rf = 3.6 ÷ 8.0 = 0.45 (1)\nDo not award: an answer with units` },
      { q: `Ethylamine can be prepared by heating bromoethane with ammonia in ethanol in a sealed tube:\nCH₃CH₂Br + 2NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + NH₄Br\n\nExplain why a large excess of ammonia is used.`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `to reduce further substitution / to reduce the ethylamine reacting with more bromoethane to form secondary and tertiary amines (or quaternary ammonium salts) (1)\nAllow: to make it more likely that bromoethane collides with ammonia rather than with ethylamine\nIgnore: "to increase the yield" alone` },
      { q: `A Grignard reagent, CH₃CH₂MgBr, is prepared by adding bromoethane to magnesium turnings in dry ethoxyethane. All the apparatus is dried in an oven beforehand.\n\nExplain why the apparatus and solvent must be dry.`, marks: 1, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Grignard reagent reacts with / is decomposed by water (forming ethane) (1)\nAllow: CH₃CH₂MgBr + H₂O → CH₃CH₃ + Mg(OH)Br\nIgnore: "water would react" without reference to the Grignard reagent` },
      { q: `1,4-Dimethylbenzene has two methyl groups on opposite carbon atoms of a benzene ring.\n\nDeduce the number of peaks in the ¹³C NMR spectrum of 1,4-dimethylbenzene.`, marks: 1, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `3 (1)\n(CH₃ carbons; ring carbons bonded to CH₃; ring carbons bonded to H)` },
      { q: `A student tests three separate liquids by warming each with iodine in the presence of sodium hydroxide solution:\npropan-2-ol  propanal  propanone\n\nIdentify the liquids that give a pale yellow precipitate and give the name of the precipitate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `propan-2-ol and propanone (both needed, no extra) (1)\ntriiodomethane / iodoform / CHI₃ (1)\nAllow: second mark for name or formula independently of first mark` },
      { q: `A student uses Tollens' reagent to distinguish between butanal and butanone. Each liquid is added to freshly prepared Tollens' reagent and the tubes are warmed in a water bath.\n\nDescribe what the student would see with each liquid and explain the difference.`, marks: 2, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `butanal gives a silver mirror / grey-black precipitate and butanone gives no visible change (1)\nbutanal is oxidised (to butanoate / butanoic acid) but butanone cannot be oxidised (as there is no H on the carbonyl carbon) (1)\nAllow: silver(I) ions / [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺ reduced to silver by butanal\nDo not award: "no reaction" for observation with butanone` },
      { q: `Propanoic acid is reduced to propan-1-ol using lithium tetrahydridoaluminate(III), LiAlH₄, in dry ethoxyethane.\n\nWrite an equation for this reaction. Use [H] to represent the reducing agent and show structural formulae for the organic compounds.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `CH₃CH₂COOH and CH₃CH₂CH₂OH correctly shown as reactant and product (1)\n+ 4[H] and + H₂O, so equation balanced (1)\nCH₃CH₂COOH + 4[H] → CH₃CH₂CH₂OH + H₂O\nAllow: displayed or skeletal formulae\nDo not award: second mark if H₂O is missing` },
      { q: `The enthalpy change of hydrogenation of cyclohexene is −120 kJ mol⁻¹.\nThe enthalpy change of hydrogenation of benzene to cyclohexane is −208 kJ mol⁻¹.\n\nDeduce what these values show about the stability of benzene compared with the Kekulé structure, cyclohexa-1,3,5-triene. Include a calculation in your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `expected value for Kekulé structure = 3 × (−120) = −360 kJ mol⁻¹ (1)\nbenzene is 152 kJ mol⁻¹ more (energetically) stable than the Kekulé structure because of the delocalised π electrons (1)\nAllow: "less energy released, so benzene more stable" with reference to delocalisation\nTE on incorrect expected value` },
      { q: `A few drops of phenol are added to bromine water at room temperature. No catalyst is needed.\n\nState what is observed and give the name of the organic product.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `bromine water decolourised / turns from orange to colourless and a white precipitate forms (both needed) (1)\n2,4,6-tribromophenol (1)\nAllow: 2,4,6-tribromohydroxybenzene\nIgnore: misty fumes / HCl` },
      { q: `Ammonia, ethylamine and phenylamine are all bases. Phenylamine is a much weaker base than ammonia.\n\nExplain why phenylamine is a weaker base than ammonia.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `lone pair (of electrons) on the nitrogen atom is delocalised into the benzene ring / overlaps with the ring π system (1)\nso the lone pair is less available to accept a proton / form a dative bond with H⁺ (1)\nDo not award: second mark if the lone pair is said to be on the ring` },
      { q: `A student adds butylamine solution dropwise to aqueous copper(II) sulfate. A pale blue precipitate forms first. With excess butylamine, the precipitate dissolves to give a deep blue solution.\n\nIdentify the pale blue precipitate and explain why it forms.`, marks: 2, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `copper(II) hydroxide / Cu(OH)₂ / [Cu(H₂O)₄(OH)₂] (1)\nbutylamine reacts with water to form hydroxide ions / C₄H₉NH₂ + H₂O ⇌ C₄H₉NH₃⁺ + OH⁻, or butylamine removes H⁺ from the aqua complex (1)\nIgnore: identity of the deep blue complex` },
      { q: `2-Aminopropanoic acid (alanine), H₂NCH(CH₃)COOH, is a white crystalline solid that decomposes rather than melts on strong heating. Propanoic acid, which has a similar molar mass, is a liquid at room temperature.\n\nExplain why 2-aminopropanoic acid has a much higher melting temperature than propanoic acid.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `2-aminopropanoic acid exists as a zwitterion, ⁺H₃NCH(CH₃)COO⁻ (1)\n(so there are) strong ionic attractions between zwitterions, which are stronger than the hydrogen bonds / London forces between propanoic acid molecules (1)\nAllow: structure of zwitterion drawn for first mark` },
      { q: `Ethyl ethanoate, CH₃COOCH₂CH₃, is used as a solvent in nail varnish remover.\n\nPredict the splitting pattern of each peak in the high-resolution ¹H NMR spectrum of ethyl ethanoate.`, marks: 2, command: 'Predict', markScheme: `CH₃ attached to C=O: singlet\nCH₂: quartet\nCH₃ of ethyl group: triplet\nall three correct (2)\ntwo correct (1)\nAllow: patterns linked to correct groups by labels on a structure` },
      { q: `A chemist uses gas chromatography to analyse a mixture of three esters. The chromatogram shows three peaks with retention times of 2.4 min, 3.1 min and 5.8 min.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nState what is meant by retention time and suggest how the chemist could identify the ester responsible for each peak.`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `time taken from injection of the sample to (the component) reaching the detector (1)\ncompare with retention times of known / pure esters run under the same conditions (same column, temperature and gas flow rate) OR pass each component into a mass spectrometer (GC–MS) and compare with a database (1)\nIgnore: "time spent in the column" without reference to injection/detection` },
      { q: `Rubbish containing a polyester, made from benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid and ethane-1,2-diol, slowly breaks down in contact with alkaline water. Poly(ethene) does not break down under the same conditions.\n\nExplain this difference.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the polyester contains (polar) ester linkages which can be hydrolysed / attacked by OH⁻ ions or water (1)\npoly(ethene) contains only (non-polar) C–C and C–H bonds which are not attacked by nucleophiles / poly(ethene) is a saturated alkane-like chain (1)\nIgnore: "biodegradable" alone` },
      { q: `A student measures the melting temperature of a sample of aspirin they have prepared, using melting temperature apparatus. The sample melts between 128 °C and 132 °C. The melting temperature of pure aspirin is 136 °C.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nExplain what these results show about the student's sample.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `the sample is impure (1)\nbecause it melts below the value for pure aspirin and over a (wide) range rather than sharply (1)\nDo not award: second mark for only one of "lower" or "over a range"` },
      { q: `Ethanoyl chloride reacts vigorously with methylamine, CH₃NH₂, at room temperature. Two moles of methylamine react with each mole of ethanoyl chloride.\n\nWrite an equation for this reaction.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write', markScheme: `N-methylethanamide, CH₃CONHCH₃, as organic product (1)\nCH₃COCl + 2CH₃NH₂ → CH₃CONHCH₃ + CH₃NH₃⁺Cl⁻ (1)\nAllow: CH₃NH₃Cl\nAllow: one mark for CH₃COCl + CH₃NH₂ → CH₃CONHCH₃ + HCl` },
      { q: `A single enantiomer of 3-bromo-3-methylhexane, CH₃CH₂CBr(CH₃)CH₂CH₂CH₃, rotates the plane of plane-polarised light. It is hydrolysed by warming with aqueous sodium hydroxide. The alcohol produced, 3-methylhexan-3-ol, has no effect on plane-polarised light.\n\nExplain how this evidence shows that the hydrolysis occurs by an SN1 mechanism rather than an SN2 mechanism.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(in SN1) the C–Br bond breaks first to form a (tertiary) carbocation which is planar (around the positive carbon) (1)\nOH⁻ can attack the carbocation equally from either side / from above and below the plane (1)\nso an equimolar mixture of the two enantiomers / a racemic mixture forms whose rotations cancel; SN2 would give a single (inverted) enantiomer which would rotate plane-polarised light (1)\nDo not award: "the carbocation is optically inactive" for the third mark without reference to the mixture of enantiomers` },
      { q: `Ethyl ethanoate can be prepared from ethanol by two different routes:\nRoute 1: ethanol + ethanoic acid\nRoute 2: ethanol + ethanoyl chloride\n\nCompare the two routes in terms of the conditions needed and the yield of ester.`, marks: 3, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `Route 1 needs a (concentrated sulfuric) acid catalyst and heating under reflux (1)\nRoute 2 occurs (rapidly) at room temperature without a catalyst (1)\nRoute 1 is reversible / reaches equilibrium so the yield is lower, whereas Route 2 goes to completion so the yield is higher (1)\nAllow: Route 2 produces (toxic) HCl fumes / ethanoyl chloride reacts violently with water as a comparison point instead of either of the first two marks, max 1` },
      { q: `Phenylethanone, C₆H₅COCH₃, is made by reacting benzene with ethanoyl chloride in the presence of aluminium chloride.\n\nWrite an equation for the formation of the electrophile, and an equation for the overall reaction. State the role of the aluminium chloride.`, marks: 3, command: 'Write', markScheme: `CH₃COCl + AlCl₃ → CH₃CO⁺ + AlCl₄⁻ (1)\nC₆H₆ + CH₃COCl → C₆H₅COCH₃ + HCl (1)\n(halogen carrier) catalyst / Lewis acid / electron-pair acceptor (1)\nAllow: CH₃C⁺O for the electrophile\nIgnore: state symbols` },
      { q: `Propanoic acid can be made from bromoethane in a three-stage synthesis using a Grignard reagent.\nbromoethane → ethylmagnesium bromide → intermediate → propanoic acid\n\nState the reagents and conditions for each stage.`, marks: 3, command: 'State', markScheme: `stage 1: magnesium in dry ether / dry ethoxyethane (1)\nstage 2: (solid) carbon dioxide / dry ice (1)\nstage 3: dilute (hydrochloric or sulfuric) acid (1)\nAllow: "dry" for "anhydrous"\nDo not award: stage 1 mark if water or aqueous conditions are given` },
      { q: `Methylmagnesium bromide, CH₃MgBr, reacts with propanal in dry ether. The product is then treated with dilute acid to form an alcohol, Q.\n\nIdentify Q, classify it as primary, secondary or tertiary, and explain whether a sample of Q made in this way would rotate plane-polarised light.`, marks: 3, command: 'Identify', markScheme: `Q is butan-2-ol / CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CH₃ (1)\nsecondary (1)\nQ is chiral but the methyl group attacks the planar C=O equally from either side so a racemic mixture forms and there is no rotation (1)\nTE on incorrect Q for second mark only` },
      { q: `Compound J has the molecular formula C₃H₆O₂. Its IR spectrum has a strong absorption at 1740 cm⁻¹ and no broad absorption between 2500 and 3300 cm⁻¹.\nThe ¹H NMR spectrum of J is summarised:\n\nChemical shift / ppm | Splitting | Relative peak area\n2.0 | singlet | 3\n3.7 | singlet | 3\n\nDeduce the structure of J, explaining how you used the data.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `1740 cm⁻¹ shows C=O (of an ester) and absence of broad absorption shows no O–H, so not a carboxylic acid (1)\ntwo singlets in ratio 3:3 show two CH₃ groups in different environments, neither on a carbon next to a carbon bearing H / no adjacent H (1)\nJ is methyl ethanoate, CH₃COOCH₃ (1)\nAllow: 3.7 ppm peak shows CH₃ attached to O\nDo not award: third mark for ethyl methanoate or propanoic acid` },
      { q: `A student uses thin-layer chromatography to check a crude sample of aspirin. Spots of crude aspirin, pure aspirin and pure salicylic acid (2-hydroxybenzoic acid) are placed on a pencil base line. The plate is placed in a beaker containing solvent below the base line, and a lid is put on the beaker.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nSample | Rf value(s) of spots\ncrude aspirin | 0.45, 0.30\npure aspirin | 0.45\nsalicylic acid | 0.30\n\nDeduce what the results show about the crude sample, and explain why the base line is drawn in pencil and why the lid is used.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `crude aspirin contains (unreacted) salicylic acid because it has a spot with the same Rf as salicylic acid, 0.30 (1)\npencil (graphite) does not dissolve in / move with the solvent, whereas ink would (1)\nlid keeps the atmosphere saturated with solvent vapour / prevents evaporation of solvent from the plate (1)\nIgnore: lid "for safety" alone` },
      { q: `The dipeptide glycylalanine, H₂NCH₂CONHCH(CH₃)COOH, is heated under reflux with 6 mol dm⁻³ hydrochloric acid for several hours.\n\nGive the structures of the two organic products and explain why their amino groups are not shown as NH₂.`, marks: 3, command: 'Give', markScheme: `⁺H₃NCH₂COOH (1)\n⁺H₃NCH(CH₃)COOH (1)\nthe amine groups are basic and accept protons / are protonated in excess (strong) acid (1)\nAllow: Cl⁻ salts, e.g. ClH₃NCH₂COOH\nAllow: one mark for H₂NCH₂COOH and H₂NCH(CH₃)COOH` },
      { q: `Methyl benzoate is heated under reflux with excess aqueous sodium hydroxide until the oily layer disappears. The mixture is cooled and dilute hydrochloric acid is added until the mixture is acidic. A white solid forms.\n\nExplain these observations. Include the name of each organic product.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `ester is hydrolysed to sodium benzoate and methanol, which are soluble so the oily layer disappears (1)\nthe acid protonates benzoate ions / C₆H₅COO⁻ + H⁺ → C₆H₅COOH (1)\nbenzoic acid is (only slightly) soluble in cold water so it forms a white precipitate (1)\nAllow: equation C₆H₅COOCH₃ + NaOH → C₆H₅COONa + CH₃OH for first mark` },
      { q: `In the laboratory, the reaction of propanone with hydrogen cyanide is carried out by adding potassium cyanide solution to propanone, with the mixture kept slightly acidic using dilute sulfuric acid.\n\nExplain why potassium cyanide is used rather than hydrogen cyanide, and why the mixture is kept slightly acidic.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `HCN is a (highly) toxic gas / volatile so is hazardous to handle (1)\nHCN is a weak acid so provides few CN⁻ ions; KCN provides (a high concentration of) the CN⁻ nucleophile (1)\nH⁺ ions are needed to protonate the (negatively charged) intermediate / to form HCN in situ (1)\nIgnore: "KCN is safer" without a reason` },
      { q: `Three isomers with the molecular formula C₅H₁₀O are:\npentan-2-one, CH₃COCH₂CH₂CH₃\npentan-3-one, CH₃CH₂COCH₂CH₃\n3-methylbutan-2-one, CH₃COCH(CH₃)₂\n\nDeduce the number of peaks in the ¹³C NMR spectrum of each isomer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `pentan-2-one: 5 (1)\npentan-3-one: 3 (1)\n3-methylbutan-2-one: 4 (1)` },
      { q: `Ethanal reacts with hydrogen cyanide, in the presence of cyanide ions, to form 2-hydroxypropanenitrile, CH₃CH(OH)CN.\n\nDraw the mechanism for this reaction. Include curly arrows, and relevant lone pairs and dipoles.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `dipole on C=O (Cδ+, Oδ−) and curly arrow from lone pair on the carbon of :CN⁻ to the carbonyl carbon (1)\ncurly arrow from the C=O π bond to the oxygen atom (1)\nintermediate CH₃CH(O⁻)CN with negative charge / lone pair on oxygen (1)\ncurly arrow from lone pair on O⁻ to the H of H–CN, with curly arrow from the H–C bond to C, giving CH₃CH(OH)CN and regenerating CN⁻ (1)\nAllow: last step with H⁺ (from H₃O⁺) instead of HCN\nDo not award: first mark if arrow starts from the N of cyanide` },
      { q: `Benzene reacts with bromine in the presence of iron(III) bromide to form bromobenzene and hydrogen bromide.\n\nWrite an equation for the formation of the electrophile. Draw the mechanism for the reaction of this electrophile with benzene. Include curly arrows and the structure of the intermediate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Br₂ + FeBr₃ → Br⁺ + FeBr₄⁻ (1)\ncurly arrow from the delocalised ring (inside the hexagon) to Br⁺ (1)\nintermediate with horseshoe of partial delocalisation facing the tetrahedral carbon, positive charge inside the horseshoe, and H and Br on that carbon (1)\ncurly arrow from the C–H bond back into the ring, giving bromobenzene + H⁺ (H⁺ + FeBr₄⁻ → HBr + FeBr₃) (1)\nDo not award: third mark if horseshoe covers the sp³ carbon\nAllow: Br–Br polarised by FeBr₃ as electrophile` },
      { q: `Compound K has the molecular formula C₄H₈O₂ and is a sweet-smelling liquid.\n(data written for this practice question)\nIR spectrum: strong absorption at 1742 cm⁻¹; no broad absorption above 2500 cm⁻¹.\nMass spectrum: molecular ion peak at m/z = 88; major peak at m/z = 43.\n¹H NMR spectrum:\n\nChemical shift / ppm | Splitting | Relative peak area\n1.2 | triplet | 3\n2.0 | singlet | 3\n4.1 | quartet | 2\n\nDeduce the structure of K. Justify your answer using all of the data.`, marks: 4, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `IR: C=O present, no O–H, so K is an ester (1)\nNMR: triplet (3H) and quartet (2H) show a CH₂CH₃ group; CH₂ at 4.1 ppm is bonded to O (1)\nsinglet (3H) at 2.0 ppm shows CH₃ with no adjacent H, next to C=O; m/z = 43 is CH₃CO⁺ (1)\nK is ethyl ethanoate, CH₃COOCH₂CH₃ (1)\nDo not award: fourth mark for methyl propanoate (would give CH₃ singlet at about 3.7 ppm and peak at m/z 57)` },
      { q: `A student prepares aspirin (C₉H₈O₄) by reacting 2.00 g of salicylic acid (C₇H₆O₃) with an excess of ethanoic anhydride. After recrystallisation, the mass of dry aspirin obtained is 1.82 g.\n(data written for this practice question)\nC₇H₆O₃ + (CH₃CO)₂O → C₉H₈O₄ + CH₃COOH\n\nCalculate the percentage yield of aspirin. Give your answer to an appropriate number of significant figures.\n[Mr: C₇H₆O₃ = 138.0; C₉H₈O₄ = 180.0]`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `moles of salicylic acid = 2.00 ÷ 138.0 = 0.01449 mol (1)\ntheoretical mass of aspirin = 0.01449 × 180.0 = 2.609 g (1)\npercentage yield = 1.82 ÷ 2.609 × 100 (1)\n= 69.8 % (3 s.f.) (1)\nAllow: moles of aspirin = 1.82 ÷ 180.0 = 0.01011 mol, then 0.01011 ÷ 0.01449 × 100\nTE throughout\nCorrect answer with no working scores full marks\nDo not award: final mark if answer is not given to 2 or 3 s.f.` },
      { q: `Nylon-6,6 can be made in the laboratory by reacting hexanedioyl dichloride, ClCO(CH₂)₄COCl, with hexane-1,6-diamine, H₂N(CH₂)₆NH₂.\n\nDraw the repeat unit of nylon-6,6 and give the formula of the other product of the reaction.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `amide linkage shown fully displayed, –CO–NH– (1)\ncorrect carbon chains: (CH₂)₄ between the two C=O carbons and (CH₂)₆ between the two N atoms (1)\none repeat unit only, with continuation bonds at both ends: –[CO(CH₂)₄CONH(CH₂)₆NH]– (1)\nHCl (1)\nAllow: repeat unit starting at a different point if correct\nDo not award: third mark if Cl or H is left on the end of the unit` },
      { q: `A student prepares ethyl ethanoate by heating ethanol and ethanoic acid under reflux with a few drops of concentrated sulfuric acid. The impure ester is then distilled off. The distillate contains ethyl ethanoate together with some ethanoic acid, ethanol and water.\n\nDescribe how the student could obtain a pure, dry sample of ethyl ethanoate from the distillate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `shake with sodium carbonate / sodium hydrogencarbonate solution in a separating funnel, releasing the pressure / gas (CO₂) regularly (to remove acid) (1)\nrun off and discard the aqueous (lower) layer, keeping the organic (upper) layer (1)\nadd anhydrous calcium chloride / magnesium sulfate / sodium sulfate and swirl until the liquid is clear, then decant / filter (1)\nredistil and collect the fraction boiling at the boiling temperature of ethyl ethanoate (about 77 °C) (1)\nAllow: washing with water to remove ethanol\nIgnore: references to reflux` },
      { q: `A technician has four unlabelled bottles. Each contains one of the following liquids:\npropan-1-ol  propanal  propanone  propanoic acid\n\nDescribe chemical tests the technician could use to identify each liquid. Include the expected observations.`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `add sodium carbonate / sodium hydrogencarbonate: effervescence only with propanoic acid (1)\nadd 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine (Brady's reagent): orange / yellow precipitate with propanal and propanone (1)\nwarm with Fehling's / Benedict's solution: (blue to) red-brown / brick-red precipitate with propanal only; OR Tollens' reagent: silver mirror with propanal only (1)\nwarm with acidified potassium dichromate(VI): orange to green with propan-1-ol (and propanal), no change with propanone and propanoic acid (1)\nAllow: iodine in alkali: yellow precipitate with propanone only, as an alternative to distinguish propanone from propanal\nAllow: PCl₅ gives steamy fumes with propan-1-ol and propanoic acid\nEach mark needs reagent and observation` },
      { q: `Phenylamine, C₆H₅NH₂, is used to make dyes. It is manufactured from benzene in a two-stage synthesis.\n\nDescribe how phenylamine can be made from benzene. Include the reagents and conditions for each stage and the name of the intermediate compound.`, marks: 5, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `stage 1: concentrated nitric acid and concentrated sulfuric acid (1)\ntemperature kept below about 55 °C / warm at 50 °C (to prevent further nitration) (1)\nintermediate is nitrobenzene (1)\nstage 2: tin and concentrated hydrochloric acid, heated under reflux (1)\nthen add (excess) sodium hydroxide (to release phenylamine from its salt), and separate e.g. by steam distillation (1)\nAllow: iron and concentrated HCl for tin\nAllow: catalytic hydrogenation (H₂ / Ni) for the fourth mark\nDo not award: "dilute" acids in stage 1` },
      { q: `Compound Y has the molecular formula C₄H₈O.\n(data written for this practice question)\nTest results:\n• orange precipitate with 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine\n• no change when warmed with Tollens' reagent\n• pale yellow precipitate when warmed with iodine in sodium hydroxide solution\n¹H NMR spectrum:\n\nChemical shift / ppm | Splitting | Relative peak area\n1.05 | triplet | 3\n2.15 | singlet | 3\n2.45 | quartet | 2\n\nThe ¹³C NMR spectrum of Y has 4 peaks.\n\nDeduce the structure of Y. Explain how each piece of evidence supports your answer.`, marks: 5, command: 'Deduce', markScheme: `2,4-DNPH positive and Tollens' negative: Y is a ketone (1)\niodoform test positive: Y contains a CH₃C=O group (1)\ntriplet (3H) and quartet (2H): CH₃CH₂ group with CH₂ next to C=O (2.45 ppm) (1)\nsinglet (3H) at 2.15 ppm: CH₃ with no adjacent H, attached to C=O; 4 carbon environments in ¹³C spectrum (1)\nY is butanone, CH₃COCH₂CH₃ (1)\nDo not award: final mark for butanal or 2-methylpropanal` },
      { q: `A student prepares aspirin using this method:\n(method written for this practice question)\n1. Mix 2.0 g of salicylic acid with 4 cm³ of ethanoic anhydride in a conical flask and add 5 drops of concentrated phosphoric(V) acid.\n2. Warm the flask in a water bath at 80 °C for 20 minutes.\n3. Carefully add 20 cm³ of cold water, then stand the flask in iced water until crystals form.\n4. Filter the crystals under reduced pressure and wash them with a small volume of ice-cold water.\n5. Recrystallise the crystals from the minimum volume of hot ethanol and water.\n\nExplain the purpose of the phosphoric(V) acid in step 1, and the procedures in steps 3 and 4.`, marks: 5, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `phosphoric(V) acid is a catalyst / speeds up the reaction (1)\ncold water reacts with / hydrolyses the excess ethanoic anhydride (to ethanoic acid) (1)\ncooling in iced water lowers the solubility of aspirin so more crystallises / increases the yield (1)\nfiltration under reduced pressure is faster and gives a drier solid (1)\nwashing with a small volume of ice-cold water removes soluble impurities (e.g. acids) without dissolving much aspirin (1)\nIgnore: "to purify" without explanation` },
      { q: `*In 1865 Kekulé proposed that benzene, C₆H₆, has a ring of six carbon atoms with alternating single and double bonds. This model has since been replaced by a delocalised model.\nData:\nC–C bond length in cyclohexane = 0.154 nm\nC=C bond length in cyclohexene = 0.134 nm\ncarbon–carbon bond length in benzene (all six bonds) = 0.139 nm\nΔH hydrogenation of cyclohexene = −120 kJ mol⁻¹\nΔH hydrogenation of benzene = −208 kJ mol⁻¹\n\nDiscuss the evidence that benzene does not have the Kekulé structure. Include a description of the delocalised model and how it explains the typical reactions of benzene.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 all six carbon–carbon bonds in benzene are the same length (0.139 nm), whereas the Kekulé structure would have three short C=C and three long C–C bonds\nIP2 the bond length is intermediate between C–C and C=C\nIP3 Kekulé structure would have ΔH hydrogenation of 3 × (−120) = −360 kJ mol⁻¹; actual value of −208 kJ mol⁻¹ is 152 kJ mol⁻¹ less exothermic, so benzene is more stable\nIP4 benzene does not decolourise bromine water / resists electrophilic addition, unlike alkenes\nIP5 delocalised model: each carbon uses three electrons in σ bonds; the remaining p orbitals overlap sideways to form a ring of delocalised π electrons above and below the plane\nIP6 benzene undergoes electrophilic substitution rather than addition because substitution keeps the stable delocalised ring / addition would destroy the delocalisation; π electron density is spread out, so lower than in C=C and halogen carrier is needed to make a stronger electrophile\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*Propanal, propanone and prop-2-en-1-ol, CH₂=CHCH₂OH, are isomers with the molecular formula C₃H₆O.\n\nDiscuss how infrared spectroscopy, mass spectrometry, ¹³C NMR and ¹H NMR spectroscopy could be used to distinguish between these three isomers.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 IR: propanal and propanone show a strong C=O absorption (about 1700–1740 cm⁻¹); prop-2-en-1-ol shows a broad O–H absorption (3200–3600 cm⁻¹) and C=C (about 1640 cm⁻¹) and no C=O\nIP2 mass spectrometry: all have molecular ion at m/z = 58, so fragments are needed; propanone gives a large peak at m/z = 43 (CH₃CO⁺); propanal gives m/z = 29 (CHO⁺ / C₂H₅⁺) and 57; prop-2-en-1-ol gives m/z = 31 (CH₂OH⁺)\nIP3 ¹³C NMR: propanone has 2 peaks; propanal and prop-2-en-1-ol each have 3 peaks\nIP4 ¹³C chemical shifts: C=O peak at about 190–220 ppm for propanal and propanone; prop-2-en-1-ol shows alkene carbons (about 115–140 ppm) and a C–O carbon (about 60 ppm)\nIP5 ¹H NMR: propanone gives only one peak (a singlet, 6H); propanal gives three peaks including an aldehyde H at about 9–10 ppm (a triplet)\nIP6 ¹H NMR of prop-2-en-1-ol: peaks for alkene H (about 5–6 ppm), CH₂ next to O (about 4 ppm) and O–H; relative peak areas/splitting confirm structure, e.g. propanal CH₃ triplet\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*A student has prepared a sample of crude aspirin as a slightly damp, off-white solid. The melting temperature of pure aspirin is 136 °C.\n\nDescribe how the student could purify the crude aspirin by recrystallisation, and explain how the student could then show that the purified product is pure aspirin.`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 dissolve the crude solid in the minimum volume of hot solvent (e.g. ethanol/water), so the solution is saturated\nIP2 filter the hot solution (through a pre-warmed funnel) to remove insoluble impurities\nIP3 allow the filtrate to cool slowly (then in ice) so that aspirin crystallises while soluble impurities stay in solution\nIP4 filter the crystals under reduced pressure, wash with a small volume of ice-cold solvent, and dry (e.g. between filter papers or in a desiccator)\nIP5 measure the melting temperature (e.g. with melting temperature apparatus); a pure sample melts sharply at 136 °C, an impure sample melts lower and over a range\nIP6 thin-layer chromatography alongside pure aspirin and salicylic acid: a single spot with the same Rf as pure aspirin shows the product is pure; OR compare IR spectrum with that of pure aspirin\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` },
      { q: `*Proteins are condensation polymers of 2-amino acids. Two examples of 2-amino acids are glycine, H₂NCH₂COOH, and alanine, H₂NCH(CH₃)COOH.\n\nDiscuss the structure and chemical properties of 2-amino acids, how they form peptides, and how the amino acids in a protein can be identified after hydrolysis.`, marks: 6, command: 'Discuss', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nIP1 in the solid and in aqueous solution amino acids exist as zwitterions, e.g. ⁺H₃NCH(CH₃)COO⁻, formed by internal transfer of H⁺ from COOH to NH₂\nIP2 amino acids are amphoteric: react with acids (COO⁻ / NH₂ protonated, forming ⁺H₃N–...–COOH) and with alkalis (NH₃⁺ / COOH deprotonated, forming H₂N–...–COO⁻)\nIP3 all 2-amino acids except glycine have a chiral carbon (four different groups), so solutions of naturally occurring amino acids rotate plane-polarised light; glycine has two H atoms on the α-carbon so is not optically active\nIP4 a peptide (amide) link –CONH– forms between the COOH of one amino acid and the NH₂ of another, with loss of water (condensation); two amino acids can form two different dipeptides\nIP5 hydrolysis by heating under reflux with (6 mol dm⁻³) hydrochloric acid breaks the peptide links, giving the (protonated) amino acids\nIP6 the amino acids in the hydrolysate are separated by (paper / thin-layer) chromatography, located with ninhydrin (or UV), and identified by comparing Rf values with known amino acids run under the same conditions; OR HPLC retention times compared with standards\nMarking: award marks for indicative content and for structure and reasoning.\nIndicative points seen: 6 → 4 marks; 5–4 → 3; 3–2 → 2; 1 → 1; 0 → 0.\nLinkages and fully sustained reasoning throughout: 2 marks; partially structured with some linkages: 1 mark; no linkages / unstructured: 0.\n(Reasoning marks are only available if at least two indicative points are seen.)` }
    ]
  }
};

